export const DOC_SOURCES: Record<string, string> = {
  "agents/decision": "---\ntitle: Decision agents\nsummary: Question-set agents judged by a decision provider and rolled up into one verdict — config, how to run one from a node, model ids and pinning, and what the run records.\norder: 52\n---\n\nA **decision agent** (`kind: \"decision\"`, fixed at creation) asks a decision provider a set of questions about one value and rolls the answers up into **one named verdict**. It has no prompt, no user turn, no tools, no turn loop and no reasoning toggle. It is versioned exactly like a generation agent (draft, published versions, float-to-latest, frozen into the run manifest). See [[agents]] and [[versioning]].\n\n## Config\n\n```json\n{\n  \"modelId\": \"<provider>:<model>\",\n  \"questions\": [\n    { \"id\": \"urgent\", \"type\": \"boolean\", \"prompt\": \"Is this urgent?\", \"yesMeans\": \"Mentions a deadline within 48h\", \"threshold\": 0.7 },\n    { \"id\": \"topic\", \"type\": \"category\", \"prompt\": \"What is it about?\", \"choices\": [{ \"key\": \"billing\", \"label\": \"Billing\" }, { \"key\": \"other\", \"label\": \"Other\" }] },\n    { \"id\": \"risk\", \"type\": \"scale\", \"prompt\": \"How risky?\", \"choices\": [{ \"key\": \"low\" }, { \"key\": \"medium\" }, { \"key\": \"high\" }] }\n  ],\n  \"verdicts\": [\"escalate\", \"route_billing\", \"default\"],\n  \"rules\": [\n    { \"id\": \"r1\", \"verdict\": \"escalate\", \"conditions\": [{ \"questionId\": \"urgent\", \"op\": \"is\", \"yes\": true }, { \"questionId\": \"risk\", \"op\": \"atLeast\", \"keys\": [\"medium\"] }] },\n    { \"id\": \"r2\", \"verdict\": \"route_billing\", \"conditions\": [{ \"questionId\": \"topic\", \"op\": \"equals\", \"keys\": [\"billing\"] }] },\n    { \"id\": \"fallback\", \"verdict\": \"default\", \"conditions\": [] }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n- **Question types**: `boolean` (provider returns a probability; `threshold` 0–1 is that question's yes cut; optional `yesMeans` / `noMeans` define what counts as yes and as no, for when the prompt leaves the boundary open), `category` (≥2 `choices`, provider returns a distribution), `scale` (2–10 `choices`, ordered **lowest first** — the order is the scale). A category takes at most 255 choices; past either limit the provider refuses the request. A choice's `description`, else `label`, else `key` is what the provider sees.\n- **Rules**: ordered; all of a rule's conditions must hold (AND); the **first** matching rule names the verdict; the **last** rule must have no conditions (the fallback) and only the last may. Every rule's `verdict` must be declared in `verdicts`.\n- **Condition ops by type**: boolean `gte`/`lt` (with `probability`) or `is` (with `yes`, using the question's own threshold); category `equals`/`in` (with `keys`); scale `atLeast`/`atMost` (with `keys`, compared by declared order).\n- `${variables}` in question text bind through the node's `config.inputs`, like a generation agent's.\n\n## Running one in a workflow\n\nUse an ordinary [[nodes/agent]] node: set `config.agentId` to the decision agent and bind `config.source` to the value to judge (a `ref`; without it the node judges its incoming input). The node makes one provider call (chunked sequentially if the provider caps questions per call) and outputs:\n\n```json\n{ \"answers\": { \"<questionId>\": { ... } }, \"reasoning\": \"one line per answer\", \"verdict\": \"escalate\", \"because\": \"which rule fired and what it read\" }\n```\n\nIt does **not** route by itself. Put a [[nodes/switch]] after it with `source` bound to the node's `verdict` and one case per verdict. (A generation agent with `output.kind: \"boolean\"` is different: it routes yes/no edges directly — see [[agents]].)\n\nEach `answers.<questionId>` is shaped by its question's type: a boolean has `value` (yes/no at its threshold), `probability`, `threshold`; a category has `value` (the option key); a scale has `level` (the nearest level key) and `value` (the probability-weighted index, a **number**). All three carry `confidence` and `distribution`. To route on a scale, bind `answers.<id>.level` — its `value` never equals a level key.\n\n## Providers and model ids\n\n- `modelId` is resolved by the host's decision provider (`getDecider`). Ids come from `list_decision_models` — a **different catalog** from `list_models`.\n- A host may instead turn on `decisionsViaChatModels`, which runs decisions on its chat models with structured output (\"chat-emulated\": slower, less calibrated; thresholds mean something different). A real provider always wins when both exist.\n- With neither wired, a node pointing at a decision agent fails with a clear error.\n\n## Pinning the model, and what the run records\n\n- **Pinning**: the engine passes `modelId` to the host's decider verbatim. Whether a specific, non-floating build can be chosen depends entirely on what ids the host's decision catalog offers — if it only lists a floating alias (a `-latest` style id), there is nothing to pin to from 007. The **agent version** can always be pinned (node `config.version`), but that freezes the questions and rules, not the provider's model.\n- **Recording**: each step's meta records `modelId`, `questionIds`, token usage, `agentId`, `agentName` and `agentVersion`. The recorded `modelId` is the id the provider **reported** in its response when it reports one, otherwise the configured id. So a floating alias is resolved to a concrete build in the run record only if the host's provider adapter returns it. `run_decision_preview` shows the same value as `answeredBy`.\n\n## Tooling\n\n- `create_agent({ kind: \"decision\", config })`, then `update_agent_draft` (the config is validated against the agent's stored kind). Publish with `publish_agent` (a dry run, then a confirmed publish). A draft may be incomplete, but publishing — from MCP or the editor — is refused while the matrix has any issue (a rule op its question type doesn't support, an unknown choice key, an `is` with no side, a missing fallback), because a rule that cannot hold evaluates false and silently falls through to the fallback.\n- `validate_workflow_graph` and the editor's Issues panel check a decision node against its agent's latest published matrix: every `${variable}` its questions use must be bound in `inputs`; refs into the node must name a real field (`answers.<id>` for a question that exists, and a field that question's type has); and a Switch case, or a Branch `equals`/`not_equals`, on `verdict`, a category's `.value` or a scale's `.level` must compare against a value the agent can produce. Each is an error, because none of them fails a run: the run takes a different arm instead. Nodes pinned to a version are not checked.\n- `run_decision_preview` judges one value with the draft (or `usePublished`). Read the distributions, not just the verdict.\n- Grade with a `decision_answers` eval check. See [[evals]].\n\n**Legacy `considerations`.** Questions saved before `yesMeans`/`noMeans` existed may carry `considerations`, a map of named factors. No provider reads that shape, so it never reached the model. When such a question is read, the factors are folded into the end of its `prompt` as a \"Take these into account:\" list, which every provider reads. Rewriting them into `yesMeans`/`noMeans` usually works better.\n",
  "agents/sub-agents": "---\ntitle: Sub-agents (delegation)\nsummary: How an agent spawns other agents or workflows mid-generation — the targets format, the synthesized tools, what is traced, and how evals treat them.\norder: 51\n---\n\nA generation agent can delegate work to other agents or workflows **during its own tool loop**. This is configured on the agent (`config.subAgents`), not in the graph: the sub-runs execute inline inside the primary agent node's step, and the workflow graph never shows them.\n\n## Config\n\n```json\n\"subAgents\": {\n  \"targets\": [\n    { \"kind\": \"agent\", \"id\": \"<agent id>\", \"version\": null, \"toolName\": \"spawn_researcher\", \"label\": \"Researcher\" },\n    { \"kind\": \"workflow\", \"id\": \"<workflow id>\" }\n  ],\n  \"maxConcurrent\": 4,\n  \"maxSpawns\": 10,\n  \"allowStopSignal\": true\n}\n```\n\n- `targets` — a list of **objects**, not bare ids. `kind` is `agent` or `workflow`; `id` is the agent/workflow id. `version` (agents only) is `null` to float to the latest published version or a number to pin; workflow targets always float. `toolName` (optional, `^\\w+$`) overrides the synthesized tool name, else it is `spawn_<slug of the target's name>` (duplicates get `_2`, `_3`). `label` (optional) is used in the tool's description. Empty `targets` = delegation off.\n- `maxConcurrent` (1–20, default 4) — how many sub-runs execute at once; extra spawns queue.\n- `maxSpawns` (1–50, default 10) — total spawns per execution of the node. Past it, a `spawn_*` call returns an error object (it does not throw), so the model can adapt.\n- `allowStopSignal` (default true) — gives text sub-agents a `signal_stop(reason)` tool. See below.\n\n## The tools the primary agent gets\n\nWhen `targets` is non-empty, the engine adds to the primary's tool set:\n\n- **`spawn_<name>`** per target. Non-blocking: returns `{ spawnId, target }` immediately and the sub-run starts in the background.\n  - Agent target input: `{ message, ...vars }`. `message` (string) becomes the sub-agent's **single user turn**. Each other key is an optional string for one `${variable}` found in the sub-agent's **system prompt** (`prompt`); values override run-level variables. Non-string values are JSON-stringified.\n  - Caveat: the sub-agent's own `userPrompt` template and `inputKind` are **not used** when it is spawned — `message` replaces the user turn, and variables used only in `userPrompt` are not offered on the spawn tool.\n  - Workflow target input: `{ input }`, used as the workflow's trigger payload; the workflow runs inline as a subgraph and returns its Output value.\n- **`await_subagents({ spawnIds? })`** — waits for the listed spawns (all, if omitted) and returns `{ completed[], pending[], stopped }`. Each completed entry has `status` (`completed`/`failed`), `output`, `error`, `stopSignalled`, `reason`.\n- **`check_subagents()`** — non-blocking status of every spawn.\n\nA name colliding with a registered tool in the agent's `toolIds` fails the node. Delegation is **one level deep**: a sub-agent gets its own registry tools but never delegation tools of its own.\n\n## Stop signal\n\nWith `allowStopSignal`, a text sub-agent can call `signal_stop(reason)`; an object/boolean sub-agent signals by returning a truthy `__stop` field. `await_subagents` then returns as soon as that sub-agent completes, listing the rest under `pending` with `stopped: true`. **Pending sub-runs are not cancelled** — they keep running in the background of the primary's step; the engine simply stops waiting on them.\n\n## Execution and budgets\n\n- Each sub-agent uses its **own** config: model, `reasoning`, `toolIds`, `maxTurns`, `toolTokenBudget`, `requireToolFirstTurn`, context window. Its token budget is not a share of the primary's — `maxSpawns` is the guard on total spend.\n- It shares the primary node's **wall-clock budget** (it runs inside that step) and its run context (prompt variables, eval simulation settings).\n- What the end user sees is inherited from the primary node's `informUser` — a sub-agent has no node of its own. See [[streaming]].\n- Sub-agent output is never streamed as the answer; only the primary's final text can be.\n\n## Versioning\n\nTargets are frozen into the run manifest at run start, transitively (a target's own sub-agents, a workflow target's agents), so a run replays against exact versions even if a target is republished mid-run. A target pointing at a version that does not exist fails the spawn with \"not in the run manifest\".\n\n## What the trace shows\n\nEach spawned **agent** is recorded as a child step of the primary node (node id `sub:<primaryNodeId>:<ordinal>`, with the ordinal as its item index) carrying its full trace: model, prompt, messages, every turn's tool calls with inputs and outputs, usage, `agentId`, `agentVersion`. A failed spawn records a failed child step. A spawned **workflow** records its inner nodes' steps under the primary node. In the primary's own trace, the `spawn_*` and `await_subagents` calls appear as tool calls. Use `get_run_step` on the primary node to drill in; `list_agent_calls` lists synthesized tools by name. See [[debugging]].\n\n## Evals\n\nSub-agents run under the **same** eval settings as the primary — there is no separate setting per sub-agent:\n\n- A sample's per-tool mode (`mocked`/`live`) and pinned output are keyed by **tool id**, so a pin applies to every agent that calls that tool, primary and sub-agents alike, and every call returns the same pinned output.\n- Write tools are neutralized in every mode; tools the host did not tag as read or write run for real. See [[tools]].\n- A synthesis sample stages tool results in the conversation and pins nothing. The primary keeps its tools — delegation tools included — and an unpinned mocked call returns `{}`.\n- A sample cannot pin a sub-agent's *answer* directly; pin the tools it calls instead. See [[evals]].\n",
  "agents": "---\ntitle: Agents\nsummary: What an agent is, every config field, how data reaches it, its output shapes, versioning, and how to edit one over MCP.\norder: 50\n---\n\nAn **agent** is a reusable, versioned LLM configuration (a model, prompts, tools, an output contract). Workflows never embed one: an `agent` node is a pointer at an agent by id (see [[nodes/agent]]). There are two kinds, fixed at creation and never convertible:\n\n- **generation** — a system prompt, one user turn (or a bound chat thread), an optional tool loop, and an answer. Everything on this page.\n- **decision** — a question set judged by a decision provider and rolled up into one verdict. No prompt, no tools. See [[agents/decision]].\n\n## Versions, drafts and what a run actually uses\n\n- An agent is an entity + one **draft** + immutable published **versions** (v1, v2, …).\n- An agent node's `version` is `null` by default, which **floats to the latest published version**. A number pins that exact version.\n- At run start the engine resolves every agent reachable from the graph (including sub-agent targets and agents inside called workflows, transitively) into the **run manifest** and freezes it. Publishing a new version mid-run changes nothing for that run; the next run picks it up.\n- The draft is never run by a workflow. It is what `run_agent_preview` and `run_eval` with `draftAgentId` measure.\n- **Publishing.** `create_agent` creates v1 (so a brand-new agent is immediately runnable once a node points at it). After that, `update_agent_draft` edits the draft and `publish_agent` publishes it — a dry run first (it names every workflow that will pick the version up), then a confirmed publish. See [[authoring]]. `discard_agent_draft` throws the draft away; `update_agent_draft({ fromVersion })` restores an old version into the draft for a rollback. `list_agent_versions` shows history. See [[versioning]].\n\n## How data reaches an agent\n\nThere is **no implicit channel**. An incoming edge is sequencing only; it never becomes the agent's message.\n\n- `${name}` tokens in `prompt` and `userPrompt` are the agent's **input contract** (the union of both). Each workflow node maps them in its `config.inputs` to a literal or a `ref` into an upstream node's output. Non-string values are JSON-stringified; null becomes empty. An unbound `${var}` reaches the model as the literal token (the editor flags it).\n- Run-level prompt variables supplied by the host are also available; a node-bound value overrides them.\n- `inputKind: \"task\"` (default) — the model gets exactly one user turn: the rendered `userPrompt`. It must be non-empty (save-time error otherwise).\n- `inputKind: \"conversation\"` — the node **must** bind `config.conversation` to a message list (usually the trigger's `messages`); an unbound or non-array conversation throws at run time. A non-empty `userPrompt` is appended after the thread as the current turn.\n- Spilled large values (blob refs) are rehydrated before interpolation.\n\n## Config fields\n\n| field | default | meaning |\n| ----- | ------- | ------- |\n| `modelId` | required | Composite `provider:model` id from `list_models`. |\n| `prompt` | required | System prompt. Put **instructions only** here: it is the provider's cache prefix, so per-call data in it defeats prompt caching. |\n| `userPrompt` | `\"\"` | The user turn. Required for `task`; optional for `conversation`. Per-call data goes here. |\n| `toolIds` | `[]` | Registry tool ids (from `get_tool_catalog`). Only `ai-tool` entries; a function-only tool cannot be given to an agent. See [[tools]]. |\n| `maxTurns` | 5 (1–100) | Rounds of tool calling before the model must answer. One turn never offers tools. |\n| `toolTokenBudget` | `null` | Tokens (summed across turns) the agent may spend researching; reaching it denies tools on the next turn, forcing the answer. Bounds research, not the final answer. `null` = no ceiling. |\n| `answerReservePercent` | 10 (2–50) | Share of the model's context window kept free for the answer. The loop stops calling tools when one more turn (measured from real per-turn growth) would eat into it. |\n| `requireToolFirstTurn` | false | Turn 1 must call a tool. Inert with no tools or `maxTurns: 1`. |\n| `reasoning` | false | Whether the model thinks before answering (costly; worth it for judgement and multi-step analysis, not extraction). Distinct from the node's display-only `informUser.reasoning`. Ignored by models that cannot reason. |\n| `webSearch` | `\"off\"` | Provider-side web search: `off`, `auto` (provider decides), `on` (every reply). The query leaves unscreened — use a search tool instead when that matters. |\n| `webCitations` | false | Ask the provider to footnote claims with pages read. Meaningless when `webSearch` is off. |\n| `output` | `{ kind: \"text\" }` | See below. |\n| `inputKind` | `\"task\"` | See above. |\n| `subAgents` | empty | Delegation whitelist and guardrails. See [[agents/sub-agents]]. |\n| `referenceKinds` | `[]` | Host-defined inline reference kinds this agent writes. Non-empty appends the reference grammar to the prompt and **checks every reference against what the run actually saw** (prompt, messages, every tool result including sub-agents'); an unsourced reference is stripped to its label and a warning logged. Text output only. |\n\n## Output kinds\n\n- `{ kind: \"text\" }` → node output `{ text }` (plus `references` when `referenceKinds` is set). The only kind that can stream to the end user — see [[streaming]].\n- `{ kind: \"boolean\" }` → `{ answer, confidence, reason, feedback }`. The node **also routes** its outgoing `yes`/`no` edges like a Branch, while its whole output still flows to refs downstream.\n- `{ kind: \"object\", schema }` → an object matching the JSON Schema. Strict-mode providers ignore unsupported constructs; prefer nullable over optional fields.\n\nThe node's output is only the answer. Tool calls, reasoning, the rendered prompt and token usage are recorded in the step's trace (`get_run_step`), **not** in the output — a downstream `ref` cannot read them.\n\n## Model requirements\n\nThe chosen model must support what the config needs: tool calling when `toolIds` is non-empty, structured output for `boolean`/`object`, reasoning when `reasoning` is on, web search when `webSearch` is not off. `create_agent` and `update_agent_draft` check these, plus that `modelId` and every tool id exist, before writing.\n\n## Editing over MCP\n\n- `get_agent` first, then `update_agent_draft` with the **whole** config — it replaces the draft outright. Fields easy to lose by omission: `subAgents`, `toolTokenBudget`, `answerReservePercent`, `requireToolFirstTurn`, `webCitations`, `referenceKinds`. The reply's `removed` names anything that disappeared.\n- `run_agent_preview` runs the draft once with **simulated** tools (the model invents tool results): checks prompt, format and tool choice, not correctness.\n- `run_eval({ draftAgentId })` grades the draft against its eval sets. See [[evals]].\n- `list_agent_calls` shows real executions (turns, tokens, cost, tool usage, early stops) — read it before tuning `maxTurns` or budgets.\n- `update_agent` changes name/icon/colour only; `update_description` the description.\n",
  "authoring": "---\ntitle: Authoring through the MCP\nsummary: The safe loop for changing workflows and agents from an MCP session — patch ops, validation, publishing, rollback, what you cannot do, and the mistakes that lint catches.\norder: 90\n---\n\n## Connections: read-only vs write\n\nWhich tools exist is decided by the scope the session was authorized with, not by anything you send. A read-only connection has **no** write tools registered at all — if a tool from the dictionary is missing, this session cannot write. Every write lands in the change feed (`list_changes`) attributed to the person who authorized the session, with `source: \"mcp\"` marking it as made through an AI client rather than a click. Say what you changed — your edit is attributed to them.\n\n## What you can and cannot change\n\n| thing | draft | publish | from here? |\n| ----- | ----- | ------- | ---------- |\n| workflow graph | `patch_workflow_draft`, `update_workflow_draft` | `publish_workflow` | **yes**, gated |\n| workflow name / archive | — | immediate | `update_workflow` |\n| agent config | `update_agent_draft` | — | `publish_agent` — dry run, then confirm (below) |\n| new agent | `create_agent` (seeds a published v1 nobody references) | — | yes |\n| agent name / icon / colour | — | immediate | `update_agent` |\n| description of workflow, agent, Goal, Sample; a run's note | — | immediate, unversioned | `update_description` |\n\nWhy publishing an agent takes two calls: a published agent version floats into **every** workflow that references it without a pin, so one publish can change several live workflows at once (a workflow publish changes only what its own trigger runs next). `publish_agent` without `confirm` is a dry run: it publishes nothing, lists what changed, every published workflow that will pick the new version up (`affected.floating`) and the ones pinned to an older version that won't (`affected.pinned`), and returns a `confirm` token. Show the user that list. Calling again with `confirm` and a `changeNote` publishes; the token is bound to the exact draft and live version the dry run saw, so any edit or publish in between is refused and you dry-run again. `baseVersionNumber` is the live version number, as for `publish_workflow`. The version records the authorized user as its publisher; there is no structured approver field, so name a reviewer in `changeNote` if one signed off. There is no tool to archive or delete an agent.\n\nThere is **no tool that runs an unpublished workflow draft.** Agents can be previewed and evaluated as drafts; workflows are exercised after publishing (an eval Goal on the workflow, or `retry_run`).\n\n## The workflow loop\n\n1. **Read.** `get_workflow` — the published graph (`currentVersion`) and any draft (`draft`), which can differ. Note the published `versionNumber`; you need it to publish. If a draft already exists, someone has unpublished edits in progress — publishing ships them too.\n2. **Understand** what the engine will do with the shape you plan: [[execution]], [[graph]], [[nodes]]. For a Tool node, read the tool's real argument schema with `get_tool_catalog({ toolIds: [...] })` — never guess argument names.\n3. **Edit the draft** with `patch_workflow_draft` (preferred) or `update_workflow_draft`.\n4. **Validate.** Every draft write already returns the lint; `validate_workflow_graph({ workflowId })` re-runs it on demand. Errors block publishing; warnings are advisory but usually real.\n5. **Publish.** `publish_workflow({ workflowId, baseVersionNumber, changeNote })`.\n6. **Exercise and watch.** Run an eval Goal against the workflow (`run_eval`), or `retry_run` a representative run in `restart` mode, then read it with `get_run` (see [[debugging]], [[evals]]).\n\nTo back out: `discard_workflow_draft` before publishing; after publishing, `update_workflow_draft({ fromVersion: N })` copies a known-good version into the draft, then publish that.\n\n### `patch_workflow_draft` ops\n\n`patch_workflow_draft({ workflowId, ops: [ … ] })`. Starts from the draft, or from the published graph if there is no draft. Ops apply **in order and all-or-nothing** — one bad op writes nothing. The result must still be a well-formed graph, or nothing is written. The reply lints the result, lists every node that differs from the published version, and flags `inheritedUnpublishedEdits` when the draft already differed before your patch.\n\n| op | fields | effect |\n| -- | ------ | ------ |\n| `\"add_node\"` | `kind`, `toolId` (required for `tool`), `label?`, `subgraphOf?` | adds a node with its kind's default config and **no edges**; returns its new id. `trigger` and `output` can't be added. `subgraphOf` = an iteration node's id, to add inside its per-item subgraph |\n| `\"set_tool_arg\"` | `nodeId`, `arg`, `binding` | sets one Tool-node argument: `{ \"kind\": \"literal\", \"value\": … }` (in the type the tool declares — a real boolean, not `\"false\"`) or `{ \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": …, \"path\": \"\" }` |\n| `\"remove_tool_arg\"` | `nodeId`, `arg` | removes one argument binding |\n| `\"set_node_label\"` | `nodeId`, `label` | renames the node (display only) |\n| `\"merge_node_config\"` | `nodeId`, `config` | **shallow** merge into `config`: each top-level key you send replaces that key whole; keys you don't send are kept |\n| `\"set_inform_user\"` | `nodeId`, `informUser` | what the end user sees while the step runs (see [[streaming]]); `dynamic` is agent nodes only |\n| `\"set_node_execution\"` | `nodeId`, `execution` | `{ continueOnError?, timeoutMs?, background?, retries?: { limit, delayMs?, backoff? } }`; `{}` clears it (see [[execution]]) |\n| `\"add_edge\"` | `source`, `target`, `condition?` | `condition` is a branch's `\"yes\"`/`\"no\"` or a switch case key; both ends must be in the same graph |\n| `\"remove_edge\"` | `edgeId`, or `source` + `target` | removes matching edges |\n| `\"remove_node\"` | `nodeId` | removes the node and every edge touching it |\n\nNotes:\n\n- `informUser` and `execution` are siblings of `config`, so `\"merge_node_config\"` can't reach them — use their dedicated ops.\n- Ops reach into iteration subgraphs by inner node id. An iteration subgraph is a closed scope: no edge crosses it. Feed an iteration from one node through its `config.source` ref.\n- `\"add_edge\"` cannot set `fastTrack`. To mark an edge fast-track, use `update_workflow_draft` with the full graph.\n- Positions are editor-only; new nodes are placed below the existing ones.\n- Binding the Output is a `\"merge_node_config\"` on the Output node: `{ \"source\": { \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"<answer node>\", \"path\": \"\" } }`.\n\n### `update_workflow_draft`\n\nReplaces the whole draft graph (`{ version: 1, nodes, edges }`) — anything you omit is gone. Use it only for a structural rewrite the ops can't express, or with `fromVersion: N` (no graph) to roll the draft back to an exact copy of a published version, read server-side.\n\n### `publish_workflow` gates\n\n- `baseVersionNumber` must equal the version live **right now** (`0` for a never-published workflow). If someone published since you read, it is refused — re-read, re-check your draft on top of theirs, retry.\n- The draft must lint with zero errors, and must differ from the live version.\n- `changeNote` is required (one line, like a commit subject). An AI summary of the change is generated by default (`summarize: false` skips it).\n- Runs already in flight finish on the version they started with; the next run uses the new one.\n\n### `create_workflow`\n\nCreates a workflow whose v1 is the minimal graph — the chosen trigger (`manual`, `periodic` with `cron`, or an event `kind` from `list_trigger_events`) wired straight to an Output. That v1 is **published but deliberately broken**: the Output has no value bound, which is an error, so build the graph with patches, bind the Output last, then publish v2. A `periodic` workflow starts firing on its schedule as soon as a version is published. `update_workflow({ archived: true })` retires a workflow (it stops firing; history survives).\n\n## The agent loop\n\n1. `get_agent` — check `agent.kind` (`generation` or `decision`; immutable) and read both `currentVersion.config` and `draft.config`.\n2. `update_agent_draft({ agentId, config })` — **the whole config, not a patch.** Any field you omit is lost. The ones most often lost because nothing prompts for them: `subAgents`, `toolTokenBudget`, `answerReservePercent`, `requireToolFirstTurn`, `webCitations`, `referenceKinds`. Check `removed` in the reply. The config is validated against the agent's kind, the model id against the enabled catalog, every tool id against the tool catalog, and the model's capabilities against what the config needs. `fromVersion: N` restores a published version into the draft instead.\n3. Try it: `run_agent_preview` (generation) runs the **draft** once. **All tools are simulated** — the model invents plausible results — so it checks prompt, format and tool choice, not correctness. Pass `promptVariables` for `${…}` tokens, and `messages` for a conversation agent. `run_decision_preview({ agentId, state })` runs a decision agent's draft for real (one provider call); read the per-question distributions, not just the verdict.\n4. Measure it: `run_eval({ setIds, draftAgentId })` grades the draft against the agent's Goals ([[evals]]).\n5. `publish_agent({ agentId, baseVersionNumber })` — the dry run. Put `affected` in front of the user.\n6. `publish_agent({ agentId, baseVersionNumber, confirm, changeNote })` — publishes. Or `discard_agent_draft` to throw the draft away. Roll back with `update_agent_draft({ fromVersion })` and publish again.\n\n`create_agent` makes a new agent with a published v1 and a matching draft. Generation configs need `modelId` (from `list_models`, composite `provider:model`), `prompt` and `userPrompt`; decision configs need `modelId` (from `list_decision_models`), `questions`, `verdicts`, `rules`. Nothing runs a new agent until a workflow's agent node points at it. See [[agents]], [[agents/decision]].\n\nThere is one draft row per workflow and per agent. Every draft write — and every discard — replaces whatever a person had unsaved in the editor.\n\n## Mistakes the lint catches (and some it can't)\n\n- **Output with no value bound** — error. The incoming edge decides *when* the Output fires; `config.source` decides *what* it returns.\n- **A ref to a node that isn't upstream** — warning (\"nothing connects that node to this one\"). A ref without a sequencing edge path races: the referenced node may not have run yet. Data never flows along edges; edges only order work ([[graph]]).\n- **Tool-node args out of step with the tool's schema** — error from the server-side check: an arg the tool no longer declares, a required arg unbound, a literal of the wrong type.\n- **Several parallel paths into one Output**, or a node joining both arms of a branch — errors; that join can never complete ([[execution]]).\n- **Agent node with no agent selected**, iteration with no list, text with no body, transform with no or invalid expression, switch cases without edges.\n- **Task agent with an empty `userPrompt`** — refused when the agent config is saved: the user prompt is the only way data reaches a task agent. A **conversation** agent whose node doesn't bind `conversation` fails at run time.\n- **Inform-user set on a step inside an iteration** — warning; nothing it reports reaches the user.\n\nNot caught: a ref `path` that names a field the upstream node never produces (it resolves to nothing at run time). Read the upstream node's output shape from a real run with `get_run_step` before binding into it.\n\nRelated: [[overview]], [[versioning]], [[debugging]], [[evals]].\n",
  "concepts": "---\ntitle: Dictionary\nsummary: Every noun you will meet in 007, A–Z, one or two lines each, with a pointer to the page that goes deeper.\norder: 10\n---\n\nThese are the words used in tool results, run traces and the other pages. Where an entry names a field, it is the field you will see in JSON.\n\n## A–C\n\n- **Actor**: the person a run acts for (`actorId` on the run). It is opaque text supplied by the host. Unattended runs (schedules, evals) have none. A workflow-call child run inherits it unless its trigger names a different one. See [[triggers]].\n- **Agent**: a reusable, versioned model configuration that workflow nodes point at by id. It is one of two kinds, fixed at creation: **generation** (prompt + tools → an answer) or **decision** (a set of questions → a verdict). See [[agents]] and [[agents/decision]].\n- **Agent node**: a node whose `config.agentId` points at an agent. It carries the bindings for that agent's `${variables}`, not the agent's behavior. See [[nodes/agent]].\n- **Agent version**: an immutable published snapshot of an agent's config. Nodes float to the latest unless they pin `config.version`. See [[versioning]].\n- **Aggregate**: a wait-for-all join that collects every live upstream output into a list. See [[nodes/aggregate]].\n- **Answer / Output**: the value an Output node names with `config.source`. That value is what the caller of the run receives. See [[nodes/output]].\n- **Binding**: how a node gets data. Either a `literal` value, or a `ref` (`nodeId` + dotted `path`) into an earlier node's output. Edges never carry data. See [[graph]].\n- **Blob ref**: a small pointer that stands in for a large value spilled to external storage. Nodes that read it get the real text back transparently, if the host wired a resolver. See [[limits]].\n- **Branch**: a yes/no routing node driven by a deterministic predicate, with no model call. See [[nodes/branch]].\n- **Change log** (`wf_change`): the append-only record of who changed what, and when, across workflows, agents, evals, models and connectors. It is the only who-touched-this record. Read it with `list_changes`. See [[versioning]].\n- **Check**: one assertion in an eval sample's check tree (`{ op, checks[] }`). Some checks are deterministic, others are judged by a model. See [[evals]].\n- **Child run**: a run started by another run, either a workflow-call callee or one item of a durable iteration. It is linked by `parentRunId` / `parentNodeId` / `itemIndex`. See [[triggers]] and [[nodes/workflow]].\n- **Connector**: a remote MCP server configured as a tool source. Its tools appear with ids of the form `mcp:<connector>:<tool>` and must be enabled one by one. See [[tools]].\n- **Conversation agent**: an agent with `inputKind: \"conversation\"`. It runs on a bound message thread rather than a single rendered user message. See [[agents]].\n\n## D–I\n\n- **Decision agent**: an agent whose config is a question set plus rules that roll the answers up to a named **verdict**. The node outputs `{ answers, reasoning, verdict, because }`, and a Switch usually routes on `verdict`. See [[agents/decision]].\n- **Draft**: the one editable, unpublished copy of a workflow graph or an agent config. There is one per entity, and it is overwritten on every save. Runs never execute a draft, except evals and previews that ask for it explicitly. See [[versioning]].\n- **Durable engine**: the backend that wraps every node in journaled steps. It survives eviction, retries steps and can resume from the failed node, but it cannot stream tokens. It is chosen by `engine` on the trigger node. See [[execution]].\n- **Edge**: `{ id, source, target, condition, fastTrack }`. It decides *when* a node may run (sequencing and routing), never *what* it reads. See [[graph]] and [[execution]].\n- **Engine**: `durable` or `inline`, set per workflow on its trigger (`config.engine`, absent means durable). See [[execution]].\n- **Eval run**: one launch of one or more eval sets. It is a resumable sweep with a frozen plan, not a workflow run. Each cell produces a real (eval-flagged) run. See [[evals]].\n- **Eval set / Goal**: a suite that targets one agent or workflow (floating, or pinned to a version) plus its samples. See [[evals]].\n- **Event**: a host-declared trigger kind, such as a chat message arriving. Its `inputSchema` validates the run's input. List them with `list_trigger_events`. See [[triggers]].\n- **Feature request node**: a placeholder for a future idea. It passes its input through unchanged. See [[nodes/feature-request]].\n- **Feedback**: a human thumbs rating on one answer, with triage state. Read it with `list_feedback` and `get_feedback_context`.\n- **Graph**: the workflow definition, `{ version: 1, nodes, edges }`. See [[graph]].\n- **Inline engine**: the in-process backend with one await per node and no journal. It has low latency and can stream, but no step retries and only limited resume. See [[execution]].\n- **informUser**: a per-node setting for what the end user sees while the step runs (`off` / `static` note / `dynamic` live agent activity). See [[streaming]].\n- **Iteration**: runs an embedded subgraph once per item of a list (`config.source`), in parallel up to `concurrency`. See [[nodes/iteration]].\n\n## M–R\n\n- **Manifest**: frozen at run start. It resolves every floating reference (agents, decision agents, called workflows, transitively) to the exact published version the run uses. A run never sees a publish that lands mid-run. See [[versioning]].\n- **Matrix / sweep**: an eval run across several models and/or alternate prompts and attempts, with one result per cell. See [[evals]].\n- **Model / provider**: models come from a host-supplied catalog (`list_models`). Agents reference a model by composite catalog id. A model must be enabled and capable of what the agent needs (tools, structured output, reasoning, web search).\n- **Node**: one box on the graph. It has `id`, `label`, `kind`, `config`, plus optional `informUser` and `execution` policy. The kinds are listed in [[nodes]].\n- **Note node**: a sticky note on the canvas. It never runs. See [[nodes/note]].\n- **Passthrough**: emits an author-chosen value or the forwarded input, typically to give a branch arm the same shape as its sibling before a Race. See [[nodes/passthrough]].\n- **Progress log**: the run's human-readable feed (`wf_run_log`), with entries at levels such as `progress` (user-facing) and developer levels. See [[streaming]] and [[debugging]].\n- **Publish**: turns a draft into a new immutable version, which becomes what runs. Over MCP: `publish_workflow`, and `publish_agent` (dry run first). See [[versioning]].\n- **Race**: a first-to-finish join. It fires when the first live upstream completes and passes the winner's value through. See [[nodes/race]].\n- **Ref**: a binding `{ kind: \"ref\", nodeId, path }`. It reads any earlier node's output by id, not only the node directly upstream. See [[graph]].\n- **Run** (`wf_run`): one execution of a published workflow version. Statuses: `queued`, `running`, `done` (the answer was delivered while other arms are still draining), `completed`, `failed`, `cancelled`. See [[execution]] and [[debugging]].\n- **Run note**: a free-form Markdown scratchpad on a run, shared by everyone and searchable through `list_runs`. Write it with `update_description` (kind `run`).\n\n## S–Z\n\n- **Sample**: one eval case with three parts: the input (`task` / `conversation` / `trigger`), how each tool behaves (`live`, or `mocked` with an optional pinned output; write tools never run), and the check tree. See [[evals]].\n- **Step** (`wf_run_step`): one row per node execution in a run, with input, output, meta, timing and status (`running`, `completed`, `failed`, `skipped`). Iteration items add one step per inner node per item. Read them with `get_run` and `get_run_step`. See [[debugging]].\n- **Sub-agent**: an agent or workflow another agent may launch mid-generation through synthesized `spawn_*` tools, from a whitelist in its config. See [[agents/sub-agents]].\n- **Switch**: multi-way routing that matches a value against cases, with an `else` fallback. See [[nodes/switch]].\n- **Text node**: deterministic text built by filling `${variables}` from bindings, with no model. See [[nodes/text]].\n- **Tool**: a function an agent can call, or a Tool node can call directly with no model. It is either host-registered or a connector tool. List them with `get_tool_catalog`. See [[tools]] and [[nodes/tool]].\n- **Transform**: reshapes a value with a JSONata expression. See [[nodes/transform]].\n- **Trigger**: the start node (exactly one per graph). `config.triggerKind` is `manual`, `periodic` (with `cron`) or a host event. Its output is the run's validated input. See [[triggers]] and [[nodes/trigger]].\n- **Variable** (`${name}`): a placeholder in an agent prompt or a Text body. Each one is filled from a node binding, or from run-level prompt variables. Unbound variables reach the model literally. See [[graph]].\n- **Verdict**: the named outcome a decision agent rolls up to. See [[agents/decision]].\n- **Workflow**: a named, versioned graph that runs when its trigger fires. It can be archived (it then never runs on its event). See [[versioning]].\n- **Workflow node**: calls another workflow and waits for its Output value. The callee runs as its own child run on its own engine. See [[nodes/workflow]].\n- **Workflow version**: an immutable published graph. The highest number is what runs. See [[versioning]].\n",
  "debugging": "---\ntitle: Reading runs and debugging\nsummary: How to find a run, read its trace without blowing your context, what each status means, where failures hide (child runs, iteration items, drained branches), and how to act on what you find.\norder: 92\n---\n\n## The reading path\n\n1. **Find it.** `list_runs` — newest first. Filter by `status`, `workflowId`, `triggerKind`, `workflowVersionId` (\"did failures stop after v7?\"), `since` / `until` (epoch ms), or `search` (matches workflow name, trigger kind, subject, correlation id and the run's note). Page with `limit` / `offset`. A pasted run URL or a feedback row's `runId` skips this step.\n2. **Overview.** `get_run({ runId })` — the run summary (status, error, cost, tokens, workflow), `versionNumber` / `workflowVersionId` it ran, the log feed, every step, and `children` when it called other workflows.\n3. **Drill in.** `get_run_step({ runId, cursor })` — one step with `input`, `output` and `meta` essentially whole (up to ~60k characters each). `meta` holds an agent step's full system and user prompt, its reasoning, every tool call's arguments and result, and the agent version that actually ran.\n\n`get_run` does not include the graph — read it with `get_workflow`, or `get_workflow_version` for the exact version the run used.\n\n## What `get_run` clips\n\nThe overview is deliberately lossy so it fits in context:\n\n- Each step's `input`, `output` and `meta` are cut to ~1000 characters of JSON. A clipped value turns into a **string** ending in `… [truncated N chars …]` — on any real agent step, `meta` arrives this way. Never conclude \"the field is missing\" from a clipped overview; open the step.\n- Only the last 60 steps and the last 80 log entries are returned; a leading note says how many were dropped. The tail is kept because that is where failures are. `get_run_step` can still open any step by `cursor`.\n- `logsTruncated` / `stepsPartial` mean the server itself could not return everything.\n\nPer step you get: `cursor` (the id to drill in by), `sequence` (the order the engine ran things — read a concurrent graph against this), `nodeId`, `nodeKind`, `parentNodeId`, `itemIndex`, `status`, `error`, `costUsd`, `branchResult` (the arm a branch or switch took), `startedAt` / `finishedAt` / `durationMs`, and for agent steps `agentTiming` — per turn its model time, reasoning time, tool-phase time, each tool call's duration, and a one-line `slowest`.\n\nStep statuses: `running`, `completed`, `failed`, `skipped` (an iteration item that never ran because `stopOnError` stopped the loop).\n\n## Run statuses\n\n| status | meaning |\n| ------ | ------- |\n| `queued` | created; the walk has not started |\n| `running` | nodes are executing |\n| `done` | the Output was reached — the answer is final and readable — but branches that don't feed the Output are **still running** |\n| `completed` | nothing left to run |\n| `failed` | the run failed before delivering an answer |\n| `cancelled` | in the vocabulary, but nothing in the engine currently sets it on a workflow run (eval runs use it — see `cancel_eval_run`) |\n\nA run with no side branches goes straight to `completed` without passing through `done`. Anything waiting for \"the answer\" should accept `done`. A failure in a branch that drains **after** the answer was delivered does not fail the run — the answer stands and the broken branch is recorded beside it (check the step errors). See [[execution]].\n\n### A run stuck at `queued`\n\nNo error and no log lines usually means it died before the walk began. On the durable engine, the run's references (the agents and workflows it uses) are frozen into its manifest before the run is marked `running`, and a failure there is not recorded on the run — so the run stays `queued` with nothing to read. Check that every agent the graph references exists and has a published version, and every called workflow resolves. The inline engine records such failures on the run normally.\n\n## The log feed\n\n`get_run`'s `logs` is the persisted progress feed. Levels: `node-start` / `node-end` (a node was entered / finished), `progress` (the user-facing \"what's happening\" lines — what an end user's progress view shows; see [[streaming]]), `info`, `thinking` (an agent's reasoning), `tool` (an agent called a tool), `warn`, `error`, and `state` (run-level lifecycle markers: queued, running, done, completed, failed — the only place the real settle time of a `done`-then-drain run is visible). Step errors, not log lines, are the authoritative failure detail.\n\n## Where failures hide\n\n- **Child runs.** A Workflow node's callee is its own run with its own steps. The parent trace shows only the call; the callee's insides are in `children` on `get_run` (each with `status` and `error`) — open one with `get_run(childRunId)`. A parent can read green while a child failed. Durable iteration items also run as child runs.\n- **Iteration items.** Each item's inner steps are recorded with `parentNodeId` = the iteration node's id and `itemIndex` = the item's position (top-level steps carry no parent and item index `-1`). Filter by those to read one item.\n- **Sub-agents.** A delegating agent's spawned sub-agents are recorded as child steps under the agent's node (`parentNodeId` = that node, `itemIndex` = spawn order), node id `sub:<nodeId>:<n>`, with the sub-agent's own tool calls in their `meta`. See [[agents/sub-agents]].\n- **`continueOnError` nodes** fail quietly by design: the step is `failed`, the run continues, and refs to it resolve to `null`.\n- **Thin or cut-off answers** are often budget stops, not prompt problems: `list_agent_calls({ agentId })` reports `stoppedOnTokenBudget` / `stoppedOnContextLimit` per real execution.\n\n## Cross-run views\n\n- `list_tool_invocations` — recent real calls of one tool across all runs, with arguments, results and failures. The way to spot a systematically wrong argument; compare against `get_tool_catalog({ toolIds })`.\n- `list_agent_calls` — one agent's recent real executions: turns, tokens, cost, tools called, early stops. Eval runs are excluded; an iteration fan-out folds into one row.\n- `list_trigger_events` — what each trigger kind hands a workflow (`fields`) and the shape it must return (`outputContract`).\n- `get_dashboard` — aggregate health; `list_changes` — who changed what, and when (to correlate a regression with a publish).\n\n## Feedback triage\n\n1. `list_feedback({ rating: \"down\", ackState: \"unacknowledged\" })` — end-user ratings with the `runId` that produced the rated answer.\n2. `get_feedback_context({ subjectId })` — the rating, the note and the producing run's trace in one call (`subjectId` is the rated message's id, not the run id).\n3. Fix, then turn the failure into a test: `draft_sample_from_run({ runId })` → review → `upsert_eval_sample` ([[evals]]).\n4. `triage_feedback({ subjectId, acknowledged: true, internalNote })` — marks it dealt with and records what was done (staff-only; the rater never sees it). Otherwise the same row comes back next session.\n\n## Acting on a run\n\n- **Leave the finding on the run.** `update_description({ kind: \"run\", id: runId, description })` writes the run's shared note (Markdown, last write wins, searchable from `list_runs`).\n- **Retry.** `retry_run({ runId, mode })` executes **real side effects** — it is a real run, not a dry run. `restart` (default) is a fresh run from the start on the **latest published** version — what you want after publishing a fix. `resume` keeps the original version and picks up from the failed step — for transient failures (rate limits, timeouts) where the completed work is worth keeping. Nodes that already succeeded run again under `restart`, so only idempotent nodes are safe to repeat. It refuses a run that is still `queued` / `running`, and needs the host to have wired retries.\n- The usual diagnose loop: `list_runs({ status: \"failed\" })` → `get_run` → `get_run_step` → `patch_workflow_draft` → `publish_workflow` → `retry_run` ([[authoring]]).\n\nRelated: [[execution]], [[streaming]], [[evals]], [[authoring]], [[limits]].\n",
  "evals": "---\ntitle: Evals\nsummary: Goals, Samples and checks; how tools behave under an eval (mocked, live, neutralized); staging context and documents; sweeps, judges, drafts and resumable runs.\norder: 85\n---\n\nAn eval runs a **target** (one agent, or one whole workflow) against a set of authored inputs and grades each run with deterministic checks and/or judges. The vocabulary: a **Goal** (wire name `set`) is a named batch of **Samples** (wire name `row`) aimed at one target; an **eval run** is one sweep of one or more Goals; each Sample × matrix cell produces one graded **result**.\n\n## The loop, as MCP calls\n\n1. `create_eval_set` — pick the target (`targetKind` `\"agent\"` | `\"workflow\"`, `targetId`). The target is resolved first, and the reply's `target` contract tells you exactly what a Sample must look like: `sampleInputKind`, a ready-to-fill `inputTemplate`, the agent's `toolIds` (the only legal keys for per-tool settings) and its `outputSchema` (the legal `path`s for checks). `get_eval_set` returns the same contract.\n2. `upsert_eval_sample` — one call per Sample. Omit `id` to create; pass it to edit. Editing is a patch at the top level, but `input`, `tools` and `checks` are each **replaced whole** when sent — resend the parts you want to keep. The reply carries lint `warnings` (empty variables, no checks, tool settings keyed on tools the target can't call, check paths that name no output field) and the Sample's `layer`.\n3. `run_eval` — returns an `evalRunId` immediately; the sweep runs on the server.\n4. `get_eval_run` — poll every 20–30 s until `status` is `completed`. Read `errored` separately from `failed`.\n5. Optionally `draft_sample_from_run` to mine Samples from real runs, `delete_eval_sample` to archive (and `restore: true` to bring back), `update_eval_set` to repoint, pin or archive a Goal.\n\n`list_eval_sets` and `list_eval_runs` list what exists. Everything except reads requires a write-authorized connection.\n\n## Targets\n\n- **Agent target.** The agent runs inside a hidden, auto-generated `trigger(manual) → agent → output` wrapper workflow on the **inline** engine, so it produces the same trace shape an agent node in a real workflow does. The Goal floats to the agent's latest published version unless `targetVersion` pins one. An agent with no published version has nothing to run.\n- **Decision agent target.** No graph and no run: each cell is one decision-provider call, settled immediately. Grade with `decision_answers`. A single `run_eval` cannot mix decision-agent Goals with anything else — the reports are incomparable and the call is refused.\n- **Workflow target.** The pinned (or latest) published version runs under its own trigger kind and its own `engine`. Workflow **drafts** cannot be evaluated — only published versions. Its tools are spread across many nodes, so the contract carries no `toolIds` and no `outputSchema`, and those lints don't run.\n\nA Goal floats by default, which keeps it grading what ships. Pin `targetVersion` only to reproduce a historical result, and unpin with `floatTargetVersion: true` afterwards. A pin that resolves to nothing fails the run instead of silently floating.\n\n## Sample input — one shape per target\n\n| target | `input` |\n| ------ | ------- |\n| task agent | `{ \"kind\": \"task\", \"variables\": { … } }` — values for the agent's `${variables}` |\n| conversation agent | `{ \"kind\": \"conversation\", \"turns\": [ … ], \"variables\": { … } }` |\n| workflow | `{ \"kind\": \"trigger\", \"payload\": { … }, \"variables\": { … } }` |\n| decision agent | `{ \"kind\": \"decision\", \"state\": <any JSON>, \"variables\": { … } }` |\n\n- A **conversation** Sample's `turns` become the agent's message history; the run begins mid-conversation and only the model's next reply is produced and graded. A turn is `{ \"role\": \"user\" | \"assistant\", \"text\"?, \"toolCalls\"? }`.\n- A **workflow** Sample's `payload` is the trigger payload, verbatim — the same object the host's event would have routed. `list_trigger_events` lists each event kind's `fields`; the Goal contract pre-fills them. `variables` are run-level prompt variables for agents nested in the workflow.\n- An unfilled `${var}` renders literally into the prompt.\n- Every input except `decision` also takes an optional `\"context\": { … }`: the run scope that **live** tools read (which client, firm or chat thread), keyed by the host's tool context fields. It is the same bag the agent playground collects, and the agent never sees it. A tool lists the keys it needs as `requiresContext` in `get_tool_catalog`; without them, a live scoped tool matches nothing.\n\n## Tools under an eval\n\nEvery eval run is **simulated**. How each tool behaves depends on its side-effect tag (`sideEffect` in `get_tool_catalog`) and the Sample's per-tool setting:\n\n| tool's tag | Sample says `mocked` (the default) | Sample says `live` |\n| ---------- | ---------------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| `write` | no-op, returns `{ simulated: true }` | still a no-op — **an eval never writes** |\n| `read` | returns the pinned `output`, or `{}` if nothing is pinned | executes for real |\n| untagged | **executes for real** | executes for real |\n\nUntagged tools run for real in every eval; the tag is how a tool opts in to being mocked. Connector tools are tagged from the remote server's `readOnlyHint` annotation (absent or false → `write`).\n\nThe setting is per tool: `tools: { \"fallback\": \"mocked\", \"byTool\": { \"<toolId>\": { \"mode\": \"mocked\" | \"live\", \"output\": <canned result> } } }`. A tool with no entry takes `fallback`. There is no way to take a tool away — a mocked tool with nothing pinned returns `{}`.\n\nConsequences worth knowing:\n\n- **One canned result per tool id, for every call in the run.** Fixtures are keyed by tool id only, so a tool called three times returns the same pinned output three times.\n- **The setting applies run-wide**: to agent tool calls, to Tool nodes, to sub-agents (they inherit the parent's simulate signal and fixtures), and to called child workflows (the child run inherits the parent's run context, simulate signal included).\n- A Sample's derived **layer** names what it can claim: `io` (nothing pinned, nothing live), `trajectory` (something pinned), `integration` (anything live — no longer reproducible).\n\n### Staging documents and other retrieved context\n\nPick by what you are testing:\n\n- **Pin the reading tool's result.** If the tool that reads an attachment is tagged `read`, set `byTool[\"<that tool>\"] = { \"mode\": \"mocked\", \"output\": <what it would have returned, document text included> }`. Deterministic, and the agent still has to decide to call it. Copy a real output from `get_run_step` so the shape is right.\n- **Let it run live.** `\"mode\": \"live\"` and put real ids/references in the trigger `payload` (or variables) that the tool can resolve, plus whatever `context` it `requiresContext`. Grades the retrieval end to end; the Sample becomes `integration`.\n- **Synthesis — stage it in the conversation** (conversation agents only). Add an assistant turn with `toolCalls: [{ \"tool\": \"<toolId>\", \"args\": …, \"output\": <document text> }]`. The model sees a completed tool call it can answer from, and the judges are shown these staged calls. The agent still has its tools; calling one returns `{}` unless pinned.\n- **Workflow target:** there is no per-node input injection. Document text reaches the graph only through the trigger `payload`, a pinned tool output, or a live tool.\n\nIf the reading tool is **untagged**, it runs for real no matter what the Sample says — check `get_tool_catalog` before relying on a pin.\n\n## Checks\n\n`checks: { \"op\": \"and\" | \"or\", \"checks\": [ … ] }`. Deterministic checks read the trace; judges call a model.\n\n| check | fields | passes when |\n| ----- | ------ | ----------- |\n| `tool_called` | `toolId`, `called` | the tool was (or wasn't) called |\n| `tool_args_match` | `toolId`, `path?`, `match`, `value` | some call's args match |\n| `node_visited` | `nodeId`, `visited` | the node ran (or didn't) |\n| `node_input_match` | `nodeId`, `path?`, `match`, `value` | the node's recorded input matches |\n| `output_match` | `path?`, `match`, `value` | the run output matches |\n| `decision_answers` | `verdict?`, `expect: [{ questionId, yes? \\| key?, minProbability? }]`, `nodeId?` | a decision agent's matrix matches, every miss reported. On a workflow Goal, set `nodeId` to the decision node: the workflow's output is what its Output returns, not the decision |\n| `llm_judge` | `rubric`, `path?`, `modelId?` | a chat model writes a reason, then pass/fail, then a 0–10 confidence |\n| `decision_judge` | `rubric`, `path?`, `modelId?`, `threshold?` | a decision model's P(yes) ≥ `threshold` (default 0.5) |\n\n- `match` is `equals` (deep equality), `contains` (substring, or array membership), `regex`, or `jsonpath` (currently an alias for `equals` after `path` selection). `path` is a dot/bracket path (`a.b[0].c`) into the output, args or input; omit for the whole value. A path that resolves to nothing grades `undefined`, which reads as a wrong answer.\n- **What checks can see:** the run output and the **top-level** steps — Tool nodes and each agent node's own tool calls (including the synthesized `spawn_*` / `await_subagents` calls of a delegating agent). Steps recorded under a parent — iteration items and a sub-agent's own internal tool calls — are not graded, and neither are a called child workflow's internal steps.\n- Judges are shown the graded output plus every tool call the checks can see, with outputs, plus any staged synthesis calls — so a rubric can ask about groundedness.\n- Row **status**: AND/OR over every check. **score**: the pass rate of the judge checks only (null with no judge). A Sample with **no checks** grades `error`, not pass or fail. A run that failed is not graded — its cell is `error`.\n- Prefer a deterministic check wherever one can express the assertion; use a judge only for what cannot be asserted literally. Use `decision_judge` for anything that reduces to yes/no — a probability of 0.52 is visible, a judge's \"8/10\" is not.\n\n## Mining Samples from real runs\n\n`draft_sample_from_run({ runId, layer?, cursor? })` turns one agent step of a real run into a Sample **draft** — it writes nothing. Pair with `list_feedback` (a thumbs-down row names the run). Layers:\n\n- `trajectory` (default): the run's real tool results become mocked pins, and `tool_called` checks are added for each tool used. A tool called more than once keeps only its **last** result. Large results are truncated — replace them.\n- `synthesis`: the tool results are staged as an assistant turn in the conversation and nothing is pinned. The agent keeps its tools; a call returns `{}`, so pin a tool if the answer depends on calling it again. Conversation agents only.\n\nBoth seed an `llm_judge` rubric from the run (never from its answer). Rewrite the rubric, then save with `upsert_eval_sample`. A run with several agent steps answers with the list; pass `cursor` to choose.\n\n## Running: sweeps, drafts, judges, cost\n\n`run_eval({ setIds, … })`:\n\n- `models` — sweep the target across these model ids (composite `provider:model`, from `list_models`). Each is checked against the target's needs (tools, structured output, reasoning, web search); one that can't run it is refused up front.\n- `prompts: [{ label, body }]` — alternate system prompts; the saved prompt is always included as a baseline column. Requires `models`. Not available for decision agents (their questions are the prompt).\n- `attempts` / `attemptsByModel` — best-of-N per model, to see variance. Requires `models`.\n- `draftAgentId` — grade the agent's **unsaved draft** instead of its published version (every named Goal must target that agent). The draft config is frozen into the run's plan. If the draft is identical to the published version, the reply says so.\n- `judgeModelId` — pin the judge model. Unpinned, the judge is the host default, else the first enabled catalog model — so enabling a model can silently re-grade a suite. Pin it whenever you will compare reports.\n- `concurrency` — 1, 2, 4 or 8 in flight (default 2). This is the rate the sweep hits the provider.\n\nLimits: at most **100 cells** (samples × models × prompts × attempts) per call. Each cell is a real, billed model call. Each node of an eval run is capped at a 7-minute timeout (applied run-wide, without changing the target's graph); a cell that takes longer than 15 minutes is given up on and recorded as `error`. After three consecutive provider failures a circuit breaker stops launching cells and records the rest.\n\nThe report (`get_eval_run`): per-sample verdicts with each check's reason, cost/tokens/model per cell, `matrix` (per model × prompt: pass rate, mean score, average cost, throughput, and `bestAccuracy` / `cheapest` / `fastest`), and a **drift** section comparing against the last comparable run — including whether the agent was republished since. `results` is bounded at 60 rows, worst first; narrow with `status`, `modelId`, `promptLabel`, page with `offset`, or pass `rowId` for one Sample unclipped. `pending` is cells not yet settled (or called off).\n\n## Resumable runs\n\nThe plan (every cell) is frozen on the eval run at launch, so a sweep doesn't depend on the session that started it: `run_eval` launches the first batch and returns; the host's backstop (or an attached console tab) drives the rest. If a run sits at `queued` / `running` without progress, nothing is driving it — `resume_eval_run` settles what finished and launches the next batch (safe to repeat; in-flight cells are polled, not relaunched). `cancel_eval_run` stops launching new cells; cells already started finish and are graded.\n\nRelated: [[agents]], [[agents/decision]], [[agents/sub-agents]], [[tools]], [[debugging]], [[authoring]].\n",
  "execution": "---\ntitle: How a run executes\nsummary: The scheduler's rules — when a node fires, how joins and routing work, fastTrack, done vs completed, failures, retries, timeouts and the two engines.\norder: 30\n---\n\nRead this before wiring anything with more than one incoming edge. Most \"why did X start late / never start / start too early\" questions are answered by the six rules in the first section.\n\n## The rules in one screen\n\n1. **Edges gate, refs carry data.** An edge says *when* a node may run. The value a node reads comes from a `ref` binding (`{ kind: \"ref\", nodeId, path }`) into any node that has already run. Nodes do not forward their input. See [[graph]].\n2. **An ordinary node waits for ALL its incoming edges.** It becomes ready only when every incoming edge is *alive*. An edge is alive when its source node has **completed**, and, if the edge has a `condition`, when that source's decision matched it.\n3. **A `race` node waits for ANY one incoming edge.** It fires once, on the first live edge. The Output node uses the same any-edge rule.\n4. **Everything that is ready starts at once.** There is no concurrency cap between nodes, only a per-run node budget (default 256 node executions). Dispatch is *rolling*: after each node settles, the engine re-checks for newly ready nodes and for a reachable Output.\n5. **An edge from a node that never ran stays dead forever.** A node on an untaken decision arm never runs. A node that failed never completes. Any ordinary node waiting on such an edge therefore never runs either. It is skipped, not failed.\n6. **The run's answer is released the moment an Output is reachable.** Work that doesn't feed that Output keeps running afterwards (the *drain*).\n\n## When a node becomes ready\n\n| Node | Ready when | Input it is handed |\n| ---- | ---------- | ------------------ |\n| Ordinary work node (agent, tool, text, transform, branch, switch, workflow, iteration, passthrough, feature-request) | **every** incoming edge is alive | 1 edge: that source's output. 2+ edges: an object keyed by source node id, `{ [sourceId]: output }`. |\n| `race` | **any** incoming edge is alive (fires once) | The winning source's output: the first live edge in edge-declaration order at the moment it fires. |\n| `aggregate` | **every** incoming edge is alive | An array with one element per incoming edge, in edge-declaration order. |\n| Output | **any** incoming edge is alive | n/a. The Output's value comes from its `config.source` ref, not from the edge. |\n| Trigger | seeded at start with the validated trigger input | n/a |\n| Note | never (it has no edges) | n/a |\n\nThe \"input it is handed\" column matters only for the few nodes that read their raw input when nothing is bound: a Branch or Switch with no `source`, and a Workflow node with empty `inputs`. Everything else reads data through refs.\n\n## Waiting for several nodes (\"release only after X, Y and Z\")\n\nAny ordinary node with incoming edges from X, Y and Z waits for all three. That is the default rule, and you don't need a special node for it. Choose the joining node by what you want downstream:\n\n- **`aggregate`** gives one list, `[outX, outY, outZ]`, in edge order. Use it when the next step iterates or checks the results together.\n- **Any work node** (Text, Transform, Agent, Branch…) with three incoming edges also waits for all three, and reads each value through refs. A Text node that composes the final message from `${x}`, `${y}` and `${z}` is a common gate.\n- **Do NOT point three edges straight at the Output.** An Output fires on its *first* live edge. Validation also rejects an Output that merges two or more unconditional parallel paths (\"give each parallel path its own Output node\"). Put an ordinary join node in front of the Output.\n\nTo hold the answer until checks pass, make the checks **ancestors of the Output** by edges: X, Y and Z feed a join, the join feeds a Branch or Switch on the check result, and the passing arm feeds the Output. Bind the Output's `source` to the node whose value the caller should receive. On the inline engine the node the Output binds to is the one whose tokens can stream to the reader. If that is an agent upstream of your checks, its text can reach the reader before the checks finish, so bind the Output to a node *after* the gate. See [[streaming]].\n\n## Decision routing and dead arms\n\nDecision sources set `condition` on their outgoing edges:\n\n| Source | Edge `condition` values |\n| ------ | ----------------------- |\n| `branch` | `\"yes\"` / `\"no\"`. An unconditioned edge out of a Branch is rejected, because it would fire on both results. |\n| `switch` | a case `key`, or `\"else\"` |\n| an agent whose output is Yes/No (boolean) | `\"yes\"` / `\"no\"` on conditioned edges. Its plain (unconditioned) edges always flow. |\n\nA *decision agent* (one provider call that returns a `verdict`) does **not** route. Put a Switch after it on `verdict`. See [[agents/decision]].\n\n- Only edges whose `condition` matches the decision come alive. Every other arm is dead: its nodes never run, and anything downstream of them that needs all its edges never runs either.\n- An arm with no outgoing edge, or an unmatched Switch with no `else`, **fizzles**: the path ends quietly. If no Output is ever reached and a decision did route, the run finishes with no output and no error. If no decision ever routed, the run fails as stalled.\n- **Joining both arms of one decision into an ordinary node is rejected** (`both-arms-join`): one arm is always dead, so the join would stall forever. To merge arms, use a **`race`** (it only needs one live edge), or give each arm its own Output. [[nodes/passthrough]] exists to make two arms hand a race the same shape.\n- Several Output nodes are legal, typically one per arm. Several mutually exclusive arms converging on one Output are also legal. If more than one Output is reached, the first one reached is the run's answer; if two become reachable on the same settle, node-declaration order breaks the tie. Later Outputs are recorded but don't change the answer.\n\n## Dispatch order, `background`, and `fastTrack`\n\nWhen several nodes are ready together, they all start in the same tick. Order matters only for who goes first, and in one case below (`fastTrack`) for who is held back.\n\n- **Answer-critical first.** Every Output and all its structural ancestors (over all edges, before any routing) are *answer-critical*. They are dispatched ahead of other ready nodes. This is a preference, never a gate.\n- **`execution.background: true`** removes a node from the answer-critical set, so it yields dispatch order. It still runs, and still runs concurrently. Its ancestors are not demoted. Use it for a side-effect node that happens to sit upstream of the Output.\n- **`fastTrack: true` on an edge GATES.** The *track* is each marked edge's source and target plus **every ancestor of the target**. While any track node is running or ready, ready nodes **off** the track are held: not started, just left waiting. When nothing on the track is running or ready, the held nodes start.\n\n**Why something started \"only when the answer was emitted\":** if the fast track leads to the node the Output binds to, then every off-track node is held until that node finishes. The Output then becomes reachable on that same settle and the answer is delivered immediately. Only after that do the held nodes start, in the drain. The result: any node that is not an ancestor of the fast-tracked target, such as a parallel check, cannot start until the answer is already out, so it cannot gate the answer.\n\n- A node that must run *before* the answer has to be an ancestor of the Output (connect it by edges). Ancestors of the fast-tracked target are on the track automatically.\n- A track node on an untaken decision arm never becomes ready, so it holds nothing back.\n- `fastTrack` affects only parallel scheduling. It does not change what any node reads, and it has no effect inside an inline iteration item, whose nodes run one at a time.\n\n## `done` vs `completed` (the drain)\n\n- **`done`**: an Output was reached. The answer is final and the caller is released. Before release the value is checked against the trigger's output contract (see [[triggers]]); a violation fails the run.\n- **`completed`**: every remaining arm has run itself out. Arms that don't feed the Output (a notification tool, a title update, a race loser's downstream) run in this drain phase, behind the answer.\n- A node that fails **before** the answer stops new dispatch. Nodes already running are allowed to land, since in-flight work can't be cancelled, and then the run **fails**.\n- A node that fails **after** the answer cannot retract it. The run keeps its answer, records a drain error, and stops starting new work while running siblings finish. The failed step is in the trace.\n- Anything waiting on \"the answer\" must accept `done`, not only `completed`.\n\n## Per-node `execution` policy\n\nEvery node accepts an optional `execution` object. Omitted fields fall back to the per-kind defaults below.\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n| ----- | ------- |\n| `continueOnError` | If the node still fails after its retries, record the failure and continue with output `null`. Downstream refs resolve to `null` and its edges count as completed. **Ignored on branch and switch**, because a routing decision has no safe default. |\n| `timeoutMs` | Wall-clock budget for ONE attempt. On AI kinds the agent's in-process budget is smaller (see [[limits]]); `validate_workflow_graph` warns at 210 s or less. |\n| `background` | Demote out of the answer-critical set (see above). |\n| `retries` | `{ limit, delayMs?, backoff? }`. `limit` is 0–10 retries *after* the first attempt. `backoff` is `constant`, `linear` or `exponential`. |\n\nDefaults by kind:\n\n| Kind | Timeout | Retries (durable engine only) |\n| ---- | ------- | ------------------------------ |\n| `agent`, `workflow`, `iteration` | 20 min (for an iteration, per item) | 3, 5 s delay, exponential |\n| everything else | 60 s | 2, 3 s delay, exponential |\n\nWhat is **not** retried, even on the durable engine:\n\n- a provider error the AI SDK marks non-retryable (bad request, payment required, unknown model)\n- an agent that burned its whole time budget or finished its turn loop without an answer\n- an iteration list longer than `maxItems`\n- the wait on a child run or a durable iteration item (retrying would spawn a second child)\n\nA run-scoped override (evals use one) can only **tighten** a timeout, never loosen it.\n\n## Engines: `inline` vs `durable`\n\nChosen once per workflow on the trigger node's `config.engine`. Absent means `durable`. A workflow-call callee runs on *its own* trigger's engine, never the caller's.\n\n| | `durable` | `inline` |\n| -- | -- | -- |\n| What runs it | Cloudflare Workflows, each node wrapped in journaled steps | One in-process walk inside the run's Durable Object |\n| Latency | multi-step cold start, journaling per node | low |\n| Node `retries` | applied | **not applied**: a transient blip fails the node |\n| Answer token streaming | impossible across a step boundary | possible, for the node the Output binds to ([[streaming]]) |\n| Survives eviction / deploy | yes | resumes **in place**, up to 2 times, from the run's completed steps. The interrupted node and everything after it **re-execute**, so tool nodes should be idempotent. |\n| Retry a failed run | `retry_run` with mode `resume` continues from the failed step on the pinned version; `restart` starts fresh on the latest version | only `restart`. Resuming into a new run is refused. |\n\nInteractive work someone is waiting on wants `inline`. Long, multi-stage batch work wants `durable`.\n\n## `informUser`\n\nEvery node has an `informUser` setting, which controls what the end user sees while it runs: `off`, `static` (a fixed note, `${var}` tokens allowed) or, for agents only, `dynamic` (live reasoning and/or tool-call lines). It is display-only and never changes execution. Steps inside an iteration are muted for the user; set the note on the iteration node itself. Details: [[streaming]].\n\n## Limits that stop a run\n\n- **Node budget:** 256 node executions per run by default (host-configurable). Each iteration item runs its own subgraph walk with its own budget.\n- **Stall:** no ready node, nothing running, no Output reached, and no decision ever routed means the run fails with \"Workflow stalled\".\n- More numbers: [[limits]].\n",
  "graph": "---\ntitle: The graph — shape, edges and data flow\nsummary: The workflow JSON, what edges do (sequencing, never data), how bindings and ${variables} move data, and how a graph is validated.\norder: 20\n---\n\n## TL;DR\n\n- A workflow is `{ version: 1, nodes: [...], edges: [...] }`. It has exactly one `trigger` node and at least one `output` node.\n- **Edges decide WHEN a node runs. Bindings decide WHAT it reads.** Agents, tools, Text, Transform, Branch/Switch values, Iteration lists and Outputs all read data through explicit bindings, never from an edge.\n- A `ref` binding can read **any** earlier node's output by id, not only the node directly upstream. Any node output stays readable for the rest of the run.\n- A ref also needs an edge path from the node it reads, or the read can race. See below.\n\n## Shape\n\n```json\n{\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"nodes\": [\n    { \"id\": \"t\", \"kind\": \"trigger\", \"label\": \"Start\", \"position\": {\"x\":0,\"y\":0},\n      \"config\": { \"triggerKind\": \"manual\" } },\n    { \"id\": \"a\", \"kind\": \"agent\", \"label\": \"Answer\", \"position\": {\"x\":300,\"y\":0},\n      \"config\": { \"agentId\": \"…\", \"version\": null,\n                  \"inputs\": { \"question\": { \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"t\", \"path\": \"question\" } } } },\n    { \"id\": \"o\", \"kind\": \"output\", \"label\": \"Reply\", \"position\": {\"x\":600,\"y\":0},\n      \"config\": { \"source\": { \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"a\", \"path\": \"\" } } }\n  ],\n  \"edges\": [\n    { \"id\": \"e1\", \"source\": \"t\", \"target\": \"a\", \"condition\": null },\n    { \"id\": \"e2\", \"source\": \"a\", \"target\": \"o\", \"condition\": null }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nEvery node has `id` (unique across the whole graph, *including* iteration subgraphs), `kind`, `label`, `position` (canvas only) and `config` (kind-specific; see [[nodes]]). Two fields are optional on every node: `informUser` (what the end user sees while it runs, see [[streaming]]) and `execution` (timeout, retries, best-effort, background, see [[execution]]).\n\n## Edges\n\n| field | meaning |\n| ----- | ------- |\n| `id` | Unique edge id. |\n| `source` | The node the edge leaves. |\n| `target` | The node it enters. |\n| `condition` | `null` on ordinary edges. Out of a decision node it names the arm: `\"yes\"`/`\"no\"` from a Branch (or a yes/no agent), a case key or `\"else\"` from a Switch. An unconditioned edge out of a Branch is always live, so it fires on both results, and the runtime gate rejects it. |\n| `fastTrack` | Optional. Puts the edge on the priority path: the walk finishes the marked path, and whatever it needs, before starting unrelated ready nodes. This holds work back, so it is more than a scheduling preference. See [[execution]]. |\n\nEdges carry readiness and routing only. How many incoming edges a node waits for (all of them, except Race) is covered in [[execution]].\n\n## Bindings\n\nA binding is one of two things:\n\n- `{ \"kind\": \"literal\", \"value\": <any JSON> }`: a constant.\n- `{ \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"<id>\", \"path\": \"<dotted.path>\" }`: reads that node's output. `path` walks object keys and array indexes (`documents.0.id`). An empty `path` means the whole output. A missing key resolves to `undefined`.\n\nBindings live in node config: agent `inputs` / `conversation` / `source`, tool `args`, Text and Transform `inputs`, Branch/Switch/Iteration/Output `source`, Workflow-call `inputs`, Passthrough `value`/`fields`.\n\n**What a ref can read.** The trigger's output is the run's validated input, so `{ nodeId: <trigger>, path: \"messages\" }` reads a field of the event payload. Every other node's output is documented on its page under [[nodes]]. Note that Branch and Switch output their *decision* (`{ result, reasoning }`), not the data they looked at. To read the data, ref the node that produced it.\n\n**When a ref fails at run time.** If the referenced node never ran (a branch routed around it) or is not in this graph, the consuming node fails, and the error names both nodes. A ref to a node with **no edge path** to the consumer is a lint warning (\"nothing connects that node to this one\"). It may work when the producer happens to finish first and fail when it does not, so connect it upstream.\n\n## `${variables}`\n\nAgent prompts (system and user message) and Text bodies use `${name}` placeholders. A name is letters, digits, `_` and `-`, with no spaces.\n\n- On an **agent node**, each `${name}` is filled from `config.inputs[name]` (a binding). Run-level prompt variables supplied by whoever started the run also fill names, and a node binding overrides a run-level value of the same name.\n- A non-string value is JSON-stringified, and `null`/missing becomes `\"\"`.\n- An **unbound** `${name}` reaches the model as the literal text `${name}`. The editor raises it as an issue.\n- The agent's input contract is the union of variables across its system prompt and its user message. `get_agent` lists them.\n\n## What uses the edge's value\n\nMost kinds ignore the edge-delivered value entirely. A few consume the \"forwarded input\". That value is the single upstream's output, or an object keyed by source node id when several edges are live:\n\n- **Race** passes through the winner's value, and **Aggregate** passes through the list of every live upstream's output.\n- **Passthrough** and **Feature request** can emit it unchanged.\n- **Workflow-call** with empty `inputs` hands it to the callee as its trigger input.\n- A **decision agent** node with no `source` judges it.\n\nPrefer explicit refs. They survive the graph being rewired.\n\n## Validation: two gates\n\n1. **Shape** (on every draft save). Nodes and edges must be well formed, with known kinds and valid config shapes. A draft with integrity problems still saves.\n2. **Integrity** (at publish over MCP, and when a run starts). The graph needs exactly one trigger and at least one Output, unique ids, no edges to missing nodes, and refs to existing nodes. Switch and Branch edges must carry valid conditions. Iteration subgraphs must start with an `iteration_item` trigger and contain no nested iteration. Joins must be legal:\n   - An **Output may not merge parallel paths.** Only mutually exclusive branch arms may share one Output.\n   - A **work node may not join both arms of one Branch.** It would wait forever for the dead arm.\n\n`validate_workflow_graph` lints a draft, a published version or a graph you pass in. It runs the editor's Issues checks plus the runtime gate (prefixed \"Runtime check:\"), and adds checks only the server can make: Tool-node args against the live tool catalog, agent pointers or pins that do not resolve, and decision-agent wiring (see [[agents/decision]]). Errors are runs that will fail. Warnings are runs that might. `publish_workflow` refuses while any error stands. How to edit safely is in [[authoring]].\n",
  "limits": "---\ntitle: Limits and defaults\nsummary: Every hard number an author can hit — turns, budgets, timeouts, retries, iteration widths, size caps, and how much an MCP result shows.\norder: 95\n---\n\nNumbers are this build's defaults. A host can raise a few of them (noted), and per-node `execution` settings override the timeout and retry defaults (see [[execution]]).\n\n## Agents\n\n| setting | range | default | notes |\n| ------- | ----- | ------- | ----- |\n| `maxTurns` | 1–100 | 5 | Rounds of tool calling before a final answer is forced. One turn never offers tools. The editor warns above 20. |\n| `toolTokenBudget` | ≥ 1000, or null | null (no cap) | Tokens spent *researching* before the answer is forced. The answer itself is not capped. |\n| `answerReservePercent` | 2–50 | 10 | Share of the model's context window kept free for the answer. Tool calling stops before it would be eaten. |\n| structured-output re-issue | — | 2 attempts | A malformed structured answer is re-requested once inside the node. |\n| provider retries per model call | — | 1 | On top of the first attempt. |\n| one model round trip | — | 8 min | Catches a stalled connection. |\n| one tool call inside an agent | — | 90 s | |\n| in-process budget | — | node timeout − slack, min 30 s | Slack is ¼ of the timeout, capped at 3 min: a 20 min node gets about 17 min of work, a 3 min node about 2¼ min. Hitting it aborts the node and is **not retried**. |\n\nSub-agent delegation (see [[agents/sub-agents]]):\n\n| setting | range | default |\n| ------- | ----- | ------- |\n| `maxConcurrent` | 1–20 | 4 |\n| `maxSpawns` | 1–50 | 10 |\n\n## Nodes and runs\n\n| limit | value | notes |\n| ----- | ----- | ----- |\n| Node timeout: `agent`, `workflow`, `iteration` (per item) | 20 min | Override with `execution.timeoutMs`. |\n| Node timeout: everything else | 60 s | |\n| Durable-engine step retries: AI kinds | 3, exponential from 5 s | Default when `execution.retries` is unset. |\n| Durable-engine step retries: other kinds | 2, exponential from 3 s | |\n| `execution.retries.limit` | 0–10 | Retries after the first attempt. The inline engine has no step retries. |\n| Inline engine restarts survived | 2 | After that the run is failed. |\n| Node executions per graph walk | 256 | Exceeding it fails the run. The host can raise it. |\n| Nested workflow-call depth | 16 | Reference cycles are rejected outright. |\n| Transform expression | 2 s, stack depth 200 | Per evaluation. |\n| Text body / prompts | no schema limit | The whole graph or config is stored as one JSON value per version, so the database's row size is the practical bound. |\n| Large step values | > 128 KB strings are spilled | On the durable engine, oversized strings become blob refs (if the host wired storage), because a step's result is capped near 1 MiB. Readers rehydrate them transparently. |\n| Stored error detail | 16,000 chars | Raw model text inside it: 2,000 chars. |\n| Run note | 8,000 chars | |\n\n## Iteration\n\n| setting | inline items | durable items |\n| ------- | ------------ | ------------- |\n| `maxItems` ceiling (lint) | 500 | 1000 |\n| `maxItems` default on a new node | 100 | 500 |\n| bound applied to a legacy node with none | 1000 | 1000 |\n| `concurrency` | 1–20, default 4 | same |\n| item title | 80 chars | same |\n\nA list longer than `maxItems` fails the node with a too-many-items error, which is never retried. See [[nodes/iteration]].\n\n## Evals\n\n| limit | value |\n| ----- | ----- |\n| Cells one MCP-launched sweep may request (`run_eval`) | 100 |\n| Concurrency choices | 1, 2, 4, 8 (default 2) |\n| Circuit breaker | stops after 3 consecutive cell errors |\n| Eval node timeout | 7 min per node (tightens only) |\n| How long a cell is waited on | 15 min |\n| Judge attempts | 2 |\n| Fixture size when drafting a sample from a run (`draft_sample_from_run`) | 20,000 chars per fixture |\n| Result rows returned in detail by `get_eval_run` | 60 (roll-ups always cover everything) |\n\n## What MCP results show\n\nResults are clipped so a trace fits in context. A clipped value says so. Open the narrower tool for the full value.\n\n| tool | budget |\n| ---- | ------ |\n| any single value, default | 4,000 chars |\n| `get_run` | last 60 steps, last 80 log entries, 1,000 chars per field |\n| `get_run_step` | 60,000 chars per field |\n| `validate_workflow_graph` | 40 issues listed |\n| `list_agent_calls`, `list_tool_invocations` | default 10, max 50 |\n| `list_models` | default 40, max 200 |\n| `get_dashboard` | window up to 90 days, 8 series, 10 recent failures |\n| run log feed (per read) | 500 entries |\n| feedback list | 300 rows |\n",
  "nodes/agent": "---\ntitle: Agent node\nsummary: Runs a reusable agent — pinned or floating version, `${var}` inputs, a bound conversation, decision agents — and what it outputs and streams.\norder: 62\n---\n\nAn `agent` node is a **pointer** at a reusable agent (a `wf_agent`); the prompt, model, tools and output kind live on the agent, not the node. The node says which agent, which version, and where each of the agent's inputs comes from. The same agent can be placed in many workflows with different bindings. Agent configuration itself is in [[agents]]; decision agents in [[agents/decision]].\n\n## Config\n\n| key | meaning |\n| --- | ------- |\n| `agentId` | The agent this node runs (`list_agents` / `get_agent`). Empty while unpicked in a draft; a run fails if it's empty. |\n| `version` | `null` (default) **floats** to the agent's latest published version, resolved when each run starts. A number **pins** the node to that exact published version. Either way the resolved config is frozen into the run manifest at run start, so publishing mid-run changes nothing for that run. See [[versioning]]. |\n| `inputs` | Map from each `${variable}` the agent's prompts use (system prompt **and** user message — one shared bag) to a binding: a `literal` or a `ref` into any upstream node's output. These bindings are the **only** way graph data reaches the model. Non-string values are JSON-stringified; `null`/missing become `\"\"`. A ref with `path: \"\"` passes a node's whole output. A bound input overrides a run-level variable of the same name. |\n| `conversation` | For a `conversation`-kind agent only: a binding that resolves to the message history (an array of `{ id, role, parts }` messages), usually a ref to the trigger's `messages`. **Required** for such agents — an unbound or non-array conversation throws. The agent's rendered user message, if any, is appended as the current turn. Ignored by `task` agents. |\n| `source` | For a **decision** agent only: a ref to the value its questions judge. Unset judges the node's incoming input. Ignored by generation agents. |\n\n`get_agent` shows an agent's `inputKind` and the `${variables}` it declares — bind every one. An unbound `${var}` reaches the model as the literal text `${var}` — the workflow editor flags it as a blocking issue, but nothing at run time stops it.\n\n## Output\n\nDepends on the agent's output kind ([[agents]]):\n\n| agent output kind | node output |\n| ----------------- | ----------- |\n| `text` | `{ \"text\": \"<final answer>\" }` — ref `path: \"text\"` for the bare string |\n| `object` | the object matching the agent's JSON Schema |\n| `boolean` | `{ \"answer\": true/false, \"confidence\": 0–1, \"reason\": \"…\", \"feedback\": \"…\"|null }` — and the node **routes** like a Branch: outgoing edges with `condition: \"yes\"` / `\"no\"` are taken by `answer` |\n| decision agent | `{ \"answers\": …, \"reasoning\": …, \"verdict\": …, \"because\": … }` — route on `verdict` with a downstream `switch` |\n\n## Behavior and gotchas\n\n- **No implicit input.** An incoming edge contributes nothing to the prompt; it only orders execution and makes the upstream output available to refs. If the agent needs data, bind it in `inputs`/`conversation`.\n- **Timeout:** 20 minutes per attempt by default (`execution.timeoutMs` overrides). See [[execution]].\n- **Version not in the manifest:** \"references agent … which is not in the run manifest\" means the agent (or the pinned version) has no published version. Publish the agent first.\n- **Streaming the answer:** on the `inline` engine, if this node is the one the Output's `source` names and its output kind is `text`, its answer is streamed token by token to a waiting reader as it is generated — before any downstream node runs. Structured (`object`/`boolean`) agents never stream. The durable engine never streams answers. See [[streaming]].\n- **`informUser`:** supports `off`, `static`, and `dynamic` (`reasoning` streams the model's thinking, `tools` announces each tool call). Dynamic is display-only; it never changes what the agent may call. Sub-agents spawned by this node inherit its display settings.\n- **Sub-agents:** if the agent whitelists sub-agents, the node gains synthesized `spawn_*` / `await_subagents` tools at run time. See [[agents/sub-agents]].\n- Under evals, tool calls the agent makes may be simulated or live per sample. See [[evals]].\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"reply-agent\",\n  \"kind\": \"agent\",\n  \"label\": \"Draft reply\",\n  \"position\": { \"x\": 300, \"y\": 0 },\n  \"informUser\": { \"mode\": \"dynamic\", \"reasoning\": false, \"tools\": true },\n  \"config\": {\n    \"agentId\": \"6f1c…\",\n    \"version\": null,\n    \"inputs\": {\n      \"topic\": { \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"classify\", \"path\": \"topic\" },\n      \"tone\": { \"kind\": \"literal\", \"value\": \"brief\" }\n    },\n    \"conversation\": { \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"trigger\", \"path\": \"messages\" }\n  }\n}\n```\n",
  "nodes/aggregate": "---\ntitle: Aggregate node\nsummary: Wait-for-all join — fires when every incoming edge is live and emits their outputs as one ordered list.\norder: 72\n---\n\nAn Aggregate waits for **all** of its upstreams (the ordinary readiness rule), then emits a list with one element per incoming edge. It is the wait-for-all sibling of [[nodes/race]], and the simplest answer to \"continue only after X, Y and Z have all finished\".\n\n## Config\n\nNone. `config` is `{}`.\n\n## Output\n\nAn array: `[outputOfFirstEdge, outputOfSecondEdge, …]`, in **edge-declaration order** (the order of the edges in the graph's `edges` array). It does not follow completion order or node order. Each element is that producer's whole output, never flattened. Shapes need not match. A single producer gives a one-element list. The step's meta records `count`.\n\nTo pick one result back out downstream, ref the aggregate by index (`path: \"0\"`), or ref the producer directly.\n\n## Behavior and gotchas\n\n- **Dead arms block it.** An Aggregate needs every incoming edge alive. If one producer sits on an untaken Branch/Switch arm, or failed, the Aggregate **never fires**, and neither does anything after it. It is skipped silently, not failed. Wiring both arms of one decision into it is rejected at validation. Exceptions:\n  - an upstream with `execution.continueOnError: true` that fails still completes, with output `null`, so the Aggregate fires with a `null` in that slot\n  - to collect \"whichever arm ran\", merge the arms with a Race first, then feed the Race into the Aggregate\n- To run checks in parallel and release the answer only when all pass: checks → Aggregate → Branch/Switch on the results → Output. The Output's `source` should bind a node after the gate (see [[execution]] and [[streaming]]).\n- Order is by edges, so if you rewire, check that downstream index refs (`path: \"1\"`) still point at the right producer.\n- An Aggregate is not an iteration. To run something once per element, feed the list into an [[nodes/iteration]] via its `source`.\n\n```json\n{ \"id\": \"all-checks\", \"kind\": \"aggregate\", \"label\": \"All checks\",\n  \"position\": { \"x\": 0, \"y\": 0 }, \"config\": {} }\n```\nWith edges `check-a → all-checks`, `check-b → all-checks`, `check-c → all-checks` declared in that order, the output is `[checkA, checkB, checkC]`.\n",
  "nodes/branch": "---\ntitle: Branch node\nsummary: Deterministic yes/no routing — a code predicate over one upstream value, no LLM.\norder: 69\n---\n\nA Branch tests one value with a fixed operator and routes the run down its `yes` or `no` edge. It runs in plain code: no model, no I/O, fully reproducible. For a judgement call (\"is this request in scope?\") use an agent first and branch on its result, or use a Yes/No agent, which routes on its own (see [[agents]]).\n\n## Config\n\n| Key | Type | Meaning |\n| --- | ---- | ------- |\n| `source` | ref binding, optional | The value to test, as `{ kind: \"ref\", nodeId, path }` into any node that has already run. If omitted, the node's raw incoming input is tested (see [[execution]] for what that is with several incoming edges). Prefer binding it explicitly. |\n| `operator` | enum, default `is_not_empty` | One of the operators below. |\n| `value` | any, optional | The operand. Ignored by `is_empty` and `is_not_empty`. A missing operand on a value-taking operator is flagged as an issue. |\n\n| `operator` | `yes` when… |\n| ---------- | ----------- |\n| `is_empty` | the value is `null`/missing, `\"\"`, `[]` or `{}`. `0` and `false` are **not** empty. |\n| `is_not_empty` | the opposite of `is_empty` |\n| `equals` | loose equality: if both sides parse as finite numbers they compare numerically (`\"3\"` equals `3`); otherwise they compare as strings, with objects and arrays as their JSON |\n| `not_equals` | the opposite of `equals` |\n| `contains` | the value's string form (JSON for objects) contains the operand's string form. Case-sensitive. |\n| `greater_than` / `less_than` | both sides parse as numbers and compare. Non-numeric gives `no`. |\n\nLarge values that were spilled to storage are rehydrated before the comparison, so the test sees the real text, not the storage pointer.\n\n## Output\n\n`{ result: \"yes\" | \"no\", reasoning: string }`, for example `reasoning: \"classify.label equals \\\"urgent\\\" → yes\"`. A Branch does **not** forward the data it tested. Downstream nodes that need that data should ref its producer directly.\n\n## Behavior and gotchas\n\n- Every outgoing edge must carry `condition: \"yes\"` or `\"no\"`. An edge with no condition is **rejected**: it would be live on both results, so the Branch would route nothing.\n- Leaving one arm unconnected is legal. If that result comes up, the path fizzles (see [[execution]]).\n- Nodes on the untaken arm never run. A node with edges from **both** arms is rejected because it could never fire. Merge the arms with a [[nodes/race]], or give each arm its own Output.\n- `continueOnError` is ignored on a Branch. A routing decision has no safe default, so a failure fails the run.\n- For more than two outcomes use [[nodes/switch]].\n\n```json\n{ \"id\": \"is-urgent\", \"kind\": \"branch\", \"label\": \"Urgent?\",\n  \"position\": { \"x\": 0, \"y\": 0 },\n  \"config\": { \"source\": { \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"classify\", \"path\": \"label\" },\n              \"operator\": \"equals\", \"value\": \"urgent\" } }\n```\nEdges: `{ \"source\": \"is-urgent\", \"target\": \"page-oncall\", \"condition\": \"yes\" }` and `{ \"source\": \"is-urgent\", \"target\": \"reply\", \"condition\": \"no\" }`.\n",
  "nodes/feature-request": "---\ntitle: Feature Request node\nsummary: A placeholder for a node that doesn't exist yet — records the idea and passes its input straight through.\norder: 66\n---\n\nA `feature-request` node marks a spot in a graph where the author wants a capability that 007 doesn't have yet. It does no work: it forwards its input unchanged so the run continues. The description captures the idea so it isn't lost.\n\n## Config\n\n| key | meaning |\n| --- | ------- |\n| `description` | Free text describing the wished-for behavior. An empty description is a validation warning. |\n\n## Output\n\nIts incoming input, untouched. With one live parent that is the parent's output; with several live parents it is an object keyed by parent node id (the default multi-parent join shape — see [[execution]]). The run step records the description in its meta.\n\n## Behavior and gotchas\n\n- It does not appear in run progress counts.\n- Since nodes read data by `ref`, a downstream node can usually ref the producer directly; the pass-through matters only for sequencing.\n- `informUser`: `off` or `static`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"fr-1\",\n  \"kind\": \"feature-request\",\n  \"label\": \"Dedupe results\",\n  \"position\": { \"x\": 300, \"y\": 0 },\n  \"config\": { \"description\": \"Drop search results already shown earlier in the thread.\" }\n}\n```\n",
  "nodes/iteration": "---\ntitle: Iteration node\nsummary: Run an embedded subgraph once per element of a list, in parallel up to a bound, and collect the results in order.\norder: 73\n---\n\nAn Iteration resolves a list from one upstream node and runs its own embedded **subgraph** once per element. Items run in parallel up to `concurrency`, and the output is the ordered list of per-item results. Use it for per-item LLM work: put an [[nodes/agent]] node inside the subgraph rather than writing a tool that loops.\n\n## Config\n\n| Key | Default | Meaning |\n| --- | ------- | ------- |\n| `source` | none | **Required.** A ref binding to the list, `{ kind: \"ref\", nodeId, path }`, pointing at any upstream node (for example `path: \"documents\"`). It must resolve to an array, or the node fails. It feeds from ONE node: to loop over several producers' results, put an [[nodes/aggregate]] (or a [[nodes/transform]]) in front. |\n| `concurrency` | `4` | 1–20 items in flight at once. With `inline` items this bounds the shared CPU/subrequest budget. With `durable` items it throttles what the items hit (provider rate limits, the database). |\n| `stopOnError` | `false` | `false`: a failed item's slot gets `{ \"__iterationError\": \"<message>\" }` and the rest run. `true`: the first failure stops new items from starting, waits for running ones (they are not killed), then fails the node. |\n| `itemExecution` | `inline` | How ONE item runs (see below). |\n| `itemTitle` | `\"\"` | A `${path}` template naming each item in progress lines and the run viewer, resolved against the item, for example `${title}` or `${customer.name}`. Empty means \"Item N\". |\n| `maxItems` | seeded `100` (inline) / `500` (durable) | The fence. A list longer than this fails the node **before any item runs**, and is not retried. Authoring ceiling: 500 inline, 1000 durable. Going over is flagged as an issue but still saves. A node with no value (only very old versions) runs with a permissive fallback of 1000. |\n| `subgraph` | starter subgraph | A full graph (`version`, `nodes`, `edges`) whose trigger has `triggerKind: \"iteration_item\"` and whose Output names the per-item result. Nested iterations are not allowed. |\n| `itemSchema` | none | Editor-only: the inferred JSON Schema of one element, used for binding pickers. The engine ignores it. |\n| `width` / `height` | none | Editor-only canvas size of the container. |\n\n### `itemExecution`\n\n| | `inline` | `durable` |\n| -- | -- | -- |\n| One item runs as | one unit: a single step on the durable engine, a plain await on the inline engine | its own child run (its own instance and `wf_run`) |\n| Failure partway through an item | the whole item repeats from the start, side effects included | per-node durable steps, so only the failed node retries |\n| Inner nodes' `execution` (`timeoutMs`, `retries`, `continueOnError`) | **ignored** | applied |\n| Inner nodes run | one at a time, in a simple walk | as a normal run |\n| \"Item finished\" means | the item's whole subgraph drained | the item **answered**; its background arms may still be running |\n| Cost | cheap per item | one run start per item |\n\nMany items over a small subgraph favor `inline`. A few items over an agent plus several nodes favor `durable`. The issues panel flags a subgraph that is heavy for `inline`.\n\n## Output\n\nAn array with one entry per input element, **in input order** regardless of finish order. Each entry is that item's Output value (from the subgraph Output's `source`), or `{ \"__iterationError\": \"…\" }` for a failed item when `stopOnError` is false. Step meta records `total`, `limit` and per-item status (`completed` / `failed` / `skipped`).\n\n## Behavior and gotchas\n\n- **An item sees only itself.** Inside the subgraph, the trigger's output IS the current element. Refs resolve only against nodes in the same item's subgraph; a ref to a node in the parent graph fails (\"sits outside this subgraph\"). To give items shared context, put it into each element upstream, for example with a [[nodes/transform]] that maps the list into `{ item, context }` objects.\n- Node ids must be unique across the whole tree, including inside subgraphs.\n- Per-item, per-node steps are recorded under the iteration node. In `get_run` they carry `parentNodeId` and `itemIndex`; open one with `get_run_step` by its cursor. The run viewer has an item picker. A `durable` item is also its own child run. Agents inside the subgraph are frozen into the run manifest like top-level ones.\n- `informUser` on steps **inside** the subgraph never reaches the user (it is flagged). Set `static` on the iteration node itself: its note may use `${n}` / `${total}` for the item count, and it then reports \"Processing <title> (i of n)\" per item.\n- The node's timeout (default 20 minutes) applies **per item**, not to the whole loop.\n- An empty list is not an error: the output is `[]`.\n\n```json\n{ \"id\": \"per-doc\", \"kind\": \"iteration\", \"label\": \"Summarize each document\",\n  \"position\": { \"x\": 0, \"y\": 0 },\n  \"config\": {\n    \"source\": { \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"fetch-docs\", \"path\": \"documents\" },\n    \"concurrency\": 4, \"stopOnError\": false, \"itemExecution\": \"inline\",\n    \"itemTitle\": \"${title}\", \"maxItems\": 100,\n    \"subgraph\": {\n      \"version\": 1,\n      \"nodes\": [\n        { \"id\": \"item\", \"kind\": \"trigger\", \"label\": \"Item\", \"position\": { \"x\": 0, \"y\": 0 },\n          \"config\": { \"triggerKind\": \"iteration_item\" } },\n        { \"id\": \"summarize\", \"kind\": \"agent\", \"label\": \"Summarize\", \"position\": { \"x\": 0, \"y\": 100 },\n          \"config\": { \"agentId\": \"<agent id>\", \"inputs\": { \"doc\": { \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"item\", \"path\": \"text\" } } } },\n        { \"id\": \"item-out\", \"kind\": \"output\", \"label\": \"Result\", \"position\": { \"x\": 0, \"y\": 200 },\n          \"config\": { \"source\": { \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"summarize\", \"path\": \"\" } } }\n      ],\n      \"edges\": [\n        { \"id\": \"e1\", \"source\": \"item\", \"target\": \"summarize\" },\n        { \"id\": \"e2\", \"source\": \"summarize\", \"target\": \"item-out\" }\n      ] } } }\n```\n",
  "nodes/note": "---\ntitle: Note node\nsummary: A Markdown sticky note on the canvas — never connected, never executed.\norder: 65\n---\n\nA `note` is a canvas annotation for authors. It has no ports, is never connected by an edge, and never runs; it has zero effect on execution. Use it to explain a tricky part of the graph to the next author (human or model).\n\n## Config\n\n| key | meaning |\n| --- | ------- |\n| `text` | The note's Markdown body. |\n| `width` | Editor-only width in pixels. Optional; ignored by the engine. |\n| `height` | Editor-only height in pixels. Optional; ignored by the engine. |\n\n## Output\n\nNone. A note has no output and cannot be the target of a ref.\n\n## Behavior and gotchas\n\n- Do not connect edges to or from a note.\n- `informUser` and `execution` have no effect.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"note-1\",\n  \"kind\": \"note\",\n  \"label\": \"Note\",\n  \"position\": { \"x\": 0, \"y\": 300 },\n  \"config\": { \"text\": \"The two checks below must both pass before the answer is released.\", \"width\": 320, \"height\": 120 }\n}\n```\n",
  "nodes/output": "---\ntitle: Output node\nsummary: Names the run's answer with an explicit source ref; fires on the first live incoming edge; the run is `done` there and `completed` after the rest drains.\norder: 61\n---\n\nThe `output` node ends the answer phase of a run and names **the value the caller receives**. Every workflow is created with one. The value comes from `config.source` — an explicit ref into any upstream node's output — not from whatever is wired into it. The incoming edge only decides *when* it fires.\n\n## Config\n\n| key | meaning |\n| --- | ------- |\n| `source` | A ref binding `{ \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"<id>\", \"path\": \"<path>\" }` naming the answer. Required in practice: an Output with no `source` is flagged by `validate_workflow_graph` and throws if a run reaches it. `path: \"\"` returns that node's whole output. |\n\n## Output\n\nThe resolved `source` value becomes the run's output (`wf_run.output`, what `get_run` shows, what a calling workflow receives). If the trigger declares an **output contract**, the value is parsed against it at the end of the run and the run fails if it doesn't match — e.g. a contract of `{ text }` is satisfied by binding `path: \"\"` on a text agent (whose output is `{ text }`), or `path: \"text\"` if the contract wants a bare string. A workflow entered as a callee skips the contract check; the caller gets the raw value.\n\n## Behavior and gotchas\n\n- **Fires on ANY live incoming edge, not all.** Unlike work nodes (which wait for every live parent), an Output becomes reachable as soon as one incoming edge is live. Several edges into one Output are only for converging **mutually exclusive** branch arms, where only one is ever live; an Output that merges parallel paths is a validation error (\"Merges parallel paths\"). If you need \"release only after X, Y and Z\", make the Output's only parent a node that itself depends on X, Y and Z (an `aggregate`, or any work node with all three as parents) — see [[execution]].\n- **Several Outputs are legal** (one per branch arm). The first reachable one in graph declaration order wins; the others are ignored.\n- **`done` vs `completed`.** Reaching the Output marks the run `done`: the answer is final and whoever is waiting is released immediately. Nodes that don't feed the Output keep running (the *drain*), and only then is the run `completed`. A failure during the drain does not fail the run — the answer already went out; it is reported as a drain error. Anything waiting for the answer must treat `done` as finished.\n- **Streaming.** On the `inline` engine, the agent named by `source` (the \"answer node\") streams its text to a waiting reader as it is generated — *before* the Output fires and before any later checks run. To hold an answer until checks pass, point `source` at a node downstream of the checks (for example a `text` or `passthrough` node that copies the agent's text), so the drafting agent is not the answer node. See [[streaming]].\n- A run whose routing sends every live path away from all Outputs ends with no output (an intentional \"fizzle\"); if no routing decision ever fired, that is reported as a stalled graph.\n- `informUser` and `execution` have no effect here.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"output\",\n  \"kind\": \"output\",\n  \"label\": \"Answer\",\n  \"position\": { \"x\": 900, \"y\": 0 },\n  \"config\": { \"source\": { \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"reply-agent\", \"path\": \"\" } }\n}\n```\n",
  "nodes/passthrough": "---\ntitle: Passthrough node\nsummary: Re-emits an upstream value — one binding unwrapped, an object of bindings, or its input untouched — so branch arms feed a join the same shape.\norder: 67\n---\n\nA `passthrough` node produces a value in a shape the author controls, with no LLM, tool or I/O. Its main job is converging branch arms: a Branch emits its decision, not its input, and a Race forwards whichever arm wins — so an arm that \"already has the data\" needs a node that re-surfaces that data in the same shape the sibling arm produces. It is also the simplest way to copy a value into a new node, e.g. so the Output's `source` can name a node that sits downstream of a set of checks.\n\n## Config\n\nThree modes, checked in this order. Setting both `value` and `fields` is a validation error.\n\n| key | meaning |\n| --- | ------- |\n| `value` | One binding (`ref` or `literal`). The output is that value, **unwrapped** — use it when the sibling arm emits a bare string, array or object. |\n| `fields` | Map of key → binding. The output is an object with one key per binding — use it to match a sibling's `{ name, kind }`-style shape. |\n| *(neither)* | Identity: the output is the node's incoming input. With one live parent that is the parent's output; with several, an object keyed by parent node id. |\n\n## Output\n\nAs above. Blob-spilled values are read back first, so the output holds real values.\n\n## Behavior and gotchas\n\n- A passthrough does not wait any differently from other work nodes: it fires once **every** live incoming edge is complete. That makes it a cheap \"gate\": give it the checks as parents and bind `value` to the answer, and it emits the answer only after all checks finished. See [[execution]].\n- It doesn't evaluate anything — to compute or reshape, use [[nodes/transform]]; to write text, [[nodes/text]].\n- `informUser`: `off` or `static`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"release\",\n  \"kind\": \"passthrough\",\n  \"label\": \"Release answer\",\n  \"position\": { \"x\": 700, \"y\": 0 },\n  \"config\": { \"value\": { \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"reply-agent\", \"path\": \"\" } }\n}\n```\n",
  "nodes/race": "---\ntitle: Race node\nsummary: First-to-finish join — fires once on the first live incoming edge and passes that output through; losers keep running.\norder: 71\n---\n\nA Race fires as soon as **any one** of its incoming edges is live, and passes the winning upstream's output through unchanged. It has two jobs:\n\n1. **Merging decision arms.** It is the only join allowed to take edges from both arms of a Branch or Switch. Only one arm ever runs, so the Race fires on whichever one did. This is the most common use.\n2. **First-past-the-post.** Several parallel producers of the same-shaped result feed it, and the fastest one wins.\n\n## Config\n\nNone. `config` is `{}`.\n\n## Output\n\nThe winner's output, as a single value. It is not wrapped and not keyed by node id. The winner is the first **live** incoming edge, in edge-declaration order, at the moment the Race fires. Because dispatch is rolling, that is normally the upstream that finished first; declaration order only breaks a tie between upstreams that completed on the same settle.\n\n## Losing branches: what happens to them\n\n- **They are not cancelled.** In-flight work cannot be cancelled on either engine. Every upstream that started runs to completion, side effects included: a losing tool node still sends its email or writes its record.\n- **Their results are recorded but ignored by the Race.** It has already fired, exactly once.\n- **Their downstream still runs.** A loser completes normally, so any other node fed by it becomes ready and runs.\n- **The run waits for them.** They count as pending work, so the run is `done` once the answer is out but `completed` only after the losers (and their downstream) finish. A loser that fails after the answer is a drain error, not a run failure. A loser that fails *before* the answer fails the run, like any other node failure. See [[execution]].\n- On a **decision merge**, the untaken arm never started, so there is nothing to lose.\n\nIf you need \"do A or B, never both\", route with a [[nodes/branch]] or [[nodes/switch]], not a Race. A Race only picks whose *result* to use; it doesn't stop the other work from happening.\n\n## Behavior and gotchas\n\n- All inputs should share one shape, because downstream sees whichever arrived first. [[nodes/passthrough]] reshapes one arm to match its sibling.\n- A Race seals join analysis. Validation treats paths through a Race as already merged, so a node downstream of a Race that merged both arms of a Branch is not rejected as a both-arms join.\n- A node downstream of the Race that also refs a *specific* arm's node will fail to resolve that ref when that arm didn't run. Read the Race's output instead.\n\n```json\n{ \"id\": \"merge\", \"kind\": \"race\", \"label\": \"Merge arms\",\n  \"position\": { \"x\": 0, \"y\": 0 }, \"config\": {} }\n```\nEdges: `{ \"source\": \"yes-path\", \"target\": \"merge\" }` and `{ \"source\": \"no-path\", \"target\": \"merge\" }`, both unconditioned. The `condition` lives on the Branch's own outgoing edges.\n",
  "nodes/switch": "---\ntitle: Switch node\nsummary: Multi-way deterministic routing — the first case whose value matches wins, otherwise `else`.\norder: 70\n---\n\nA Switch is the multi-way sibling of [[nodes/branch]]. It reads one value and compares it to each case **in order**. The first match picks that case's outgoing edge; if nothing matches, the `else` edge is taken. No LLM, fully reproducible. It is the normal way to route on a decision agent's `verdict` ([[agents/decision]]).\n\n## Config\n\n| Key | Type | Meaning |\n| --- | ---- | ------- |\n| `source` | ref binding, optional | The value to match. If omitted, the raw incoming input is used. Prefer binding it. |\n| `cases` | array, default `[]` | Ordered `{ key, label?, value }` entries. `key` is the routing identity, and the outgoing edge's `condition` must equal it. The editor mints `A`, `B`, `C`…; keys must be unique and may not be `else`. `label` is purely cosmetic: it is the name shown on the edge, and renaming it never re-points anything. `value` is a binding: `{ kind: \"literal\", value }` or a `ref` to another upstream value the input must equal. |\n\n## Matching rules\n\n- The comparison is the same loose equality as Branch's `equals`: numeric if both sides parse as finite numbers, otherwise string comparison (JSON for objects).\n- Cases are tested **in array order**. The first match wins, even if a later case would also match.\n- Both the subject and any ref-valued case are rehydrated if they were spilled to storage.\n- No match routes to `else`. If there is no `else` edge, that path fizzles. The editor flags a missing `else` as a warning, not an error.\n\n## Output\n\n`{ result: <winning case key or \"else\">, reasoning: string }`. Like Branch, a Switch does not forward its input.\n\n## Behavior and gotchas\n\n- Validation rejects: duplicate case keys, a case named `else`, a case with no outgoing edge, and an outgoing edge whose `condition` matches no case and isn't `else`.\n- Only the edges of the winning case come alive. Nodes on the other arms never run, and the dead-arm rules in [[execution]] apply. In particular, a node fed by two arms of the same Switch can never fire; merge arms with a [[nodes/race]].\n- `continueOnError` is ignored. A failure fails the run.\n\n```json\n{ \"id\": \"route\", \"kind\": \"switch\", \"label\": \"Route by verdict\",\n  \"position\": { \"x\": 0, \"y\": 0 },\n  \"config\": {\n    \"source\": { \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"triage\", \"path\": \"verdict\" },\n    \"cases\": [\n      { \"key\": \"A\", \"label\": \"answer\", \"value\": { \"kind\": \"literal\", \"value\": \"answer\" } },\n      { \"key\": \"B\", \"label\": \"escalate\", \"value\": { \"kind\": \"literal\", \"value\": \"escalate\" } }\n    ] } }\n```\nEdges: `condition: \"A\"`, `condition: \"B\"`, and `condition: \"else\"` for the fallback.\n",
  "nodes/text": "---\ntitle: Text node\nsummary: Writes a block of Markdown with `${values}` filled from bindings — deterministic string assembly without an LLM.\norder: 64\n---\n\nA `text` node emits a string: its `body` with every `${name}` token replaced by a bound value. It is for prose with one right answer — a message template, a prompt fragment, a fixed playbook — where spending a model call would add latency and nondeterminism. Deterministic and side-effect free.\n\n## Config\n\n| key | meaning |\n| --- | ------- |\n| `body` | Markdown text. `${name}` tokens are filled from `inputs`; everything else is emitted verbatim. |\n| `inputs` | Map from each variable name used in `body` to a binding (`literal` or `ref`). Strings are used as-is, other values are JSON-stringified, `null`/missing become `\"\"`. |\n\n## Output\n\nThe filled-in string, bare (not wrapped in an object). Downstream refs use `path: \"\"`.\n\n## Interpolation rules\n\n- A variable name is letters, digits, `_` and `-` — `${client_name}`, `${item-count}`. Spaces are not allowed: `${my var}` is not a variable and is emitted literally.\n- Markdown-escaped names such as `${my\\_var}` (what the rich-text editor stores) are read as `my_var`.\n- Only variables the body actually uses are resolved; a leftover binding the body no longer mentions is ignored.\n- There is no conditional or loop syntax. For logic, compute the value upstream (a `transform`) and bind the result.\n\n## Behavior and gotchas\n\n- **Empty body** → the node fails (\"has no text\"). `validate_workflow_graph` flags it.\n- **Unbound variable** → the node fails, naming every unbound `${token}`. It never emits a half-filled template.\n- **Size.** 007 sets no maximum length on `body`. The practical bounds are the platform's: the whole graph — every text body included — is stored as one JSON value per version, and on the durable engine a node output above 128 KB is spilled to blob storage (transparently read back by downstream bindings), with a hard failure if an output still exceeds about 768 KB. MCP reads clip long fields (`get_run_step` returns up to 60,000 characters per field). Storing long reference text (playbooks, policies) in Text nodes works; keep each well under those limits. See [[limits]].\n- **Timeout:** 60 seconds default; it is pure computation and never needs it.\n- `informUser`: `off` or `static`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"greeting\",\n  \"kind\": \"text\",\n  \"label\": \"Compose greeting\",\n  \"position\": { \"x\": 300, \"y\": 120 },\n  \"config\": {\n    \"body\": \"Hi ${name},\\n\\nWe found ${count} items that need review.\",\n    \"inputs\": {\n      \"name\": { \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"trigger\", \"path\": \"user.name\" },\n      \"count\": { \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"scan\", \"path\": \"total\" }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n",
  "nodes/tool": "---\ntitle: Tool node\nsummary: Calls one registered tool directly with bound args — no LLM; args are validated against the tool's schema.\norder: 63\n---\n\nA `tool` node calls one registered tool deterministically, with arguments bound from literals or upstream outputs. No model is involved: the same tool an agent could call is invoked directly. Use it for fetches, writes and side effects the workflow should always perform, rather than leaving them to an agent's judgment.\n\n## Config\n\n| key | meaning |\n| --- | ------- |\n| `toolId` | The registered tool's id. `get_tool_catalog` lists every tool with its input schema. Connector (remote MCP) tools have ids of the form `mcp:<connector-slug>:<tool>`. |\n| `args` | Map from each argument name to a binding (`literal` or `ref`). Each resolved value is passed as-is (not stringified), then the whole args object is validated and coerced by the tool's input schema — which also fills that schema's defaults. |\n\n## Output\n\nWhatever the tool returns, unchanged. Its shape is the tool's own contract; `get_tool_catalog` describes it where the tool declares one. The run step also records `{ toolId, args }` in its meta.\n\n## Behavior and gotchas\n\n- **Unregistered tool** → the node fails with \"Tool '…' is not registered.\" A tool can disappear from a host between versions.\n- **Arg drift.** If a tool's input schema changes after the workflow was published, the node fails at run time with a schema validation error. `validate_workflow_graph` checks every Tool node's args against the live catalog (unknown args, unbound required args, wrongly typed literals) — run it before publishing.\n- **Timeout:** 60 seconds per attempt by default; raise `execution.timeoutMs` for slow tools. See [[execution]].\n- **Retries replay the call.** `execution.retries`, durable-step replay and resuming a run can all execute the tool again. Prefer tools that are idempotent, or keep retries off for non-idempotent side effects.\n- **Evals:** under simulation, side-effecting (write) tools no-op and read tools return fixtures, unless the sample marks a tool live; the call is still recorded so a check can assert it happened. See [[evals]].\n- Large string values spilled to blob storage upstream are read back before the call, so the tool sees real values.\n- `informUser`: `off` or `static`.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"fetch-doc\",\n  \"kind\": \"tool\",\n  \"label\": \"Fetch document\",\n  \"position\": { \"x\": 300, \"y\": 0 },\n  \"config\": {\n    \"toolId\": \"fetch_document\",\n    \"args\": {\n      \"documentId\": { \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"trigger\", \"path\": \"documentId\" },\n      \"maxPages\": { \"kind\": \"literal\", \"value\": 20 }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n",
  "nodes/transform": "---\ntitle: Transform node\nsummary: Reshapes data with a JSONata expression — concatenate, map JSON to text, slice a conversation, combine several upstreams — with an optional shape check.\norder: 68\n---\n\nA `transform` node runs a [JSONata](https://docs.jsonata.org) expression over an upstream value and emits the result. Bindings can only *address* data (dotted paths); a transform *reworks* it — mapping rows to messages, joining strings, filtering, slicing, building objects. It is deterministic and side-effect free. JavaScript is not available (the runtime forbids code generation); JSONata is the whole language.\n\n## Config\n\n| key | meaning |\n| --- | ------- |\n| `source` | Binding for the value the expression walks, available as `$` (and as the implicit context). Unset → the node's incoming input (one live parent: its output; several: an object keyed by parent node id). Bind it explicitly whenever there is more than one parent. |\n| `inputs` | Map of name → binding. Each is exposed to the expression as `$name`, so one transform can combine several upstream outputs without a join node. |\n| `expression` | The JSONata expression. Compiled when the editor saves and again on every run. Empty → the node fails. |\n| `outputShape` | Optional assertion on the result. The one shape today is `conversation`: an **array** of `{ id?, role: \"system\"|\"user\"|\"assistant\", parts: [{ type, … }, …] }` with at least one part each — what a conversation agent's `conversation` binding needs. A mismatch fails the node with the offending path and a hint. |\n\n## Output\n\nThe expression's return value, as-is. No wrapping: a string expression yields a bare string (ref with `path: \"\"`).\n\n## Worked expressions\n\nThe examples assume `source` is bound to a message array (e.g. the trigger's `messages`), each message `{ id, role, parts: [{ type: \"text\", text }] }`.\n\n**Concatenate strings, with an extra input** (`inputs.heading` bound to a string):\n\n```\n$heading & \": \" & $count($) & \" messages\"\n```\n\n**Flatten a conversation to plain text:**\n\n```\n$join($.(role & \": \" & $join(parts[type=\"text\"].text, \"\")), \"\\n\")\n```\n\n**Last N messages** (here 3; safe when the thread is shorter). Note `$all := $` first — inside a `[ … ]` predicate, `$` means the current item, not the whole array:\n\n```\n($n := 3; $all := $; $start := $max([0, $count($all) - $n]);\n [$filter($all, function($v, $i) { $i >= $start })])\n```\n\n**Prior assistant turn + newest message**, as a conversation (with `outputShape: \"conversation\"`):\n\n```\n($all := $;\n $prior := $filter($all, function($v) { $v.role = \"assistant\" });\n [$count($prior) > 0 ? $prior[-1] : (), $all[-1]])\n```\n\n…or the same as one text block for a task agent's `${variable}`:\n\n```\n\"Previous answer:\\n\" & $join($[role=\"assistant\"][-1].parts[type=\"text\"].text, \"\")\n& \"\\n\\nNew message:\\n\" & $join($[-1].parts[type=\"text\"].text, \"\")\n```\n\n**Map records to messages** (rows `{ id, role, body }`):\n\n```\n[$.{ \"id\": id, \"role\": role = \"assistant\" ? \"assistant\" : \"user\",\n     \"parts\": [{ \"type\": \"text\", \"text\": body }] }]\n```\n\n## Behavior and gotchas\n\n- **Wrap list results in `[ … ]`.** JSONata collapses a one-element sequence to a bare value, so a one-message thread yields an object where longer threads yield arrays. The outer `[ … ]` array constructor keeps it an array; `outputShape: \"conversation\"` catches the mistake at this node instead of deep inside the agent.\n- **Errors fail the node:** a syntax error (with position), an evaluation error, or a shape mismatch. Refs in `source`/`inputs` to nodes with no output also throw.\n- **Guards:** recursion deeper than 200 frames is stopped; evaluation is capped at 2 seconds where the runtime clock allows (a tail-recursive infinite loop may instead run until the platform kills the step).\n- Blob-spilled upstream values are read back before evaluation.\n- `informUser`: `off` or `static`. Timeout: 60 seconds default.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"last-turns\",\n  \"kind\": \"transform\",\n  \"label\": \"Prior answer + new message\",\n  \"position\": { \"x\": 300, \"y\": 0 },\n  \"config\": {\n    \"source\": { \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"trigger\", \"path\": \"messages\" },\n    \"inputs\": {},\n    \"expression\": \"($all := $; $prior := $filter($all, function($v) { $v.role = \\\"assistant\\\" }); [$count($prior) > 0 ? $prior[-1] : (), $all[-1]])\",\n    \"outputShape\": \"conversation\"\n  }\n}\n```\n",
  "nodes/trigger": "---\ntitle: Trigger node\nsummary: How a run starts — the trigger kind, cron schedule, and which engine executes the run; its output is the run's input.\norder: 60\n---\n\nEvery graph has exactly one `trigger` node, created with the workflow; you cannot add or remove one. It does not execute: at run start the engine marks it complete and sets its output to the run's input. Every other node reaches the run input by a `ref` to the trigger's id.\n\n## Config\n\n| key | meaning |\n| --- | ------- |\n| `triggerKind` | How the workflow starts. `manual` (a person starts it; no payload schema), `periodic` (a cron schedule; requires `cron`), or a host-declared **event** kind. `list_trigger_events` lists the event kinds this host declares, with each one's input fields and output contract. `iteration_item` is reserved for the trigger inside an iteration subgraph — never author it. |\n| `cron` | Cron expression. Required when `triggerKind` is `periodic`, ignored otherwise. A periodic trigger without one fails validation. |\n| `engine` | `durable` or `inline`. Absent means `durable`. Applies to the **whole run**. `durable`: every node is a journaled step — survives eviction, retries replay, a failed run can be resumed; slower to start, and cannot stream the answer. `inline`: one process walks the graph — much lower latency, can stream the answer token by token, no step-level replay. Interactive work (chat) wants `inline`; long batch work wants `durable`. See [[execution]] and [[streaming]]. |\n\n## Output\n\nThe run's input, after validation:\n\n- **Event kinds:** the input is validated against the event's input schema before any node fires; the trigger's output is the parsed value. Ref its fields by path, e.g. `{ \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"trigger\", \"path\": \"messages\" }`.\n- **`manual` / `periodic`:** the input is passed through untouched (no schema).\n- **When this workflow is called by another workflow** (a `workflow` node), the input is seeded raw — the event schema is not re-checked. See [[nodes/workflow]].\n- **Inside an iteration subgraph** (`iteration_item`): the output is the current list element.\n\n## Behavior and gotchas\n\n- The trigger kind also decides the **output contract**: an event may declare a schema the final answer must satisfy (for example `{ text }`). The run fails at the end if the Output's value doesn't match. See [[nodes/output]] and [[triggers]].\n- Changing `engine` is a graph change — it is versioned with the workflow and takes effect for runs of the newly published version.\n- `informUser` and `execution` have no effect here.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"trigger\",\n  \"kind\": \"trigger\",\n  \"label\": \"New message\",\n  \"position\": { \"x\": 0, \"y\": 0 },\n  \"config\": { \"triggerKind\": \"chat_message\", \"engine\": \"inline\" }\n}\n```\n",
  "nodes/workflow": "---\ntitle: Workflow (call) node\nsummary: Call another published workflow as its own child run and use its Output as this node's output.\norder: 74\n---\n\nA Workflow node calls another workflow and waits for its answer. Like an agent node it is a pointer by id. It floats to the callee's **latest published version**, which is frozen into the run manifest when the parent run starts (see [[versioning]]).\n\n## Config\n\n| Key | Default | Meaning |\n| --- | ------- | ------- |\n| `workflowId` | `\"\"` | The callee's workflow id (from `list_workflows`). Empty is an authoring error. |\n| `inputs` | `{}` | The callee's trigger input. **Empty:** the node's raw incoming input is passed through as-is (a single upstream's output, or `{ [sourceId]: output }` with several edges; see [[execution]]). **Non-empty:** an object built field by field, each value a binding, `{ kind: \"literal\", value }` or `{ kind: \"ref\", nodeId, path }`. Prefer explicit `inputs`. |\n\n## Output\n\nThe callee's Output value, exactly as its Output's `source` resolved. Step meta records the callee's `workflowId`, `versionId`, `versionNumber`, `name`, and, when it ran as its own run, `childRunId` and the `engine` it ran on.\n\n## Behavior and gotchas\n\n- **The callee is always its own child run**: its own `wf_run`, linked back to the parent run and node, executing on the engine **the callee's own trigger** declares (`inline` or `durable`). The caller cannot choose the engine; there is deliberately no per-call setting. Open the child with `get_run` on `childRunId`.\n- Exception: a Workflow node inside an `inline` iteration item can't start a run, so there the callee's graph runs inline as a subgraph of the item, with no child run.\n- **Boundary checks are skipped for a callee.** Its trigger input is not validated against its trigger's input schema, and its Output is not checked against its trigger's output contract. It answers to the calling node, not to a host. Make sure the `inputs` you build match what the callee's nodes read.\n- The parent waits until the callee **answers** (reaches its Output). The callee's own background arms may still be draining when the parent moves on.\n- Reference cycles (A calls B calls A) are rejected when the run manifest is resolved, before anything executes.\n- Timeout defaults to 20 minutes, like an agent, and covers the whole callee. The wait on the child is not retried. A callee that fails fails this node, and `continueOnError` applies as usual.\n- Identity: the child derives its own actor from its trigger where the host supports that; otherwise it inherits the parent's.\n\n```json\n{ \"id\": \"call-enrich\", \"kind\": \"workflow\", \"label\": \"Enrich contact\",\n  \"position\": { \"x\": 0, \"y\": 0 },\n  \"config\": { \"workflowId\": \"<workflow id>\",\n              \"inputs\": { \"email\": { \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"trigger\", \"path\": \"email\" } } } }\n```\n",
  "nodes": "---\ntitle: Nodes\nsummary: What every node carries, how nodes are addressed by refs, the full node-kind dictionary, and which node to reach for.\norder: 59\n---\n\nA workflow graph is a list of nodes and a list of edges. **Edges carry sequencing, never data**: a node reads upstream values only through explicit bindings (a `ref` into some upstream node's output, or a `literal`). See [[graph]] for bindings and [[execution]] for when a node fires.\n\n## Fields every node carries\n\n| field | meaning |\n| ----- | ------- |\n| `id` | Stable, unique string. This is what refs, edges and the run trace use. Never reuse an id for a different node. |\n| `kind` | One of the kinds below. Decides the `config` shape. |\n| `label` | Display name. Shown in the run viewer, progress feed and error messages (\"`\"Extract text\" (tool)`\"). Required, non-empty. |\n| `position` | `{ x, y }` on the canvas. Editor-only; no effect on execution. |\n| `config` | Kind-specific. Each `nodes/<kind>` page lists every key. |\n| `informUser` | What the end user sees while this node runs: `{ mode: 'off' }` (default), `{ mode: 'static', note }` (a fixed line, may use run-level `${vars}`), or `{ mode: 'dynamic', reasoning, tools }` — **agents only** — streaming the agent's reasoning and/or tool-call announcements. See [[streaming]]. |\n| `execution` | Optional per-node policy: `continueOnError`, `timeoutMs`, `background`, `retries`. See [[execution]]. |\n\nBookend kinds (`trigger`, `output`, `note`) never execute as steps, so `informUser` and `execution` mean nothing on them. `informUser` on a node *inside* an iteration subgraph never reaches the user either — set it on the iteration node.\n\n## Addressing a node from a binding\n\nA ref binding is `{ \"kind\": \"ref\", \"nodeId\": \"<id>\", \"path\": \"<dotted path>\" }`.\n\n- `path: \"\"` means the node's **whole** output.\n- `path` walks object keys and numeric array indices: `\"documents.0.id\"`, `\"messages\"`, `\"text\"`.\n- A missing key resolves to `undefined` (no error); a ref to a node that has **not produced an output** in this run (a dead branch arm, a node not yet run) throws, naming both nodes.\n- Any upstream node's output is reachable, not only the immediate parent's. Outputs are global to the run.\n\nA literal binding is `{ \"kind\": \"literal\", \"value\": <any JSON> }`.\n\n## The node kinds\n\n{{node-kinds}}\n\n## Choosing a node\n\n| you need to… | use |\n| ------------ | --- |\n| call an LLM (generate text, a structured object, a yes/no, or a rubric decision) | `agent` → [[nodes/agent]] |\n| call a registered tool directly, no LLM | `tool` → [[nodes/tool]] |\n| write deterministic prose with `${values}` filled in | `text` → [[nodes/text]] |\n| reshape data (map rows to messages, concatenate, pick the last N items) | `transform` → [[nodes/transform]] |\n| re-emit an upstream value in a chosen shape, e.g. to make two arms match | `passthrough` → [[nodes/passthrough]] |\n| route yes/no on a deterministic test | `branch` → [[nodes/branch]] |\n| route many ways on one value | `switch` → [[nodes/switch]] |\n| wait for **all** of several producers, then continue | `aggregate` → [[nodes/aggregate]] (or simply give the next node several incoming edges — every work node already waits for all live parents) |\n| continue as soon as the **first** of several producers finishes | `race` → [[nodes/race]] |\n| run a subgraph once per list item | `iteration` → [[nodes/iteration]] |\n| call another workflow and use its answer | `workflow` → [[nodes/workflow]] |\n| name the run's answer | `output` → [[nodes/output]] |\n| leave a comment on the canvas | `note` → [[nodes/note]] |\n| park an idea for a node that doesn't exist yet | `feature-request` → [[nodes/feature-request]] |\n\n**The Output does not wait for everything.** It fires as soon as *any* of its incoming edges is live, and its value is whatever its `config.source` names. If the answer must only be released after several checks pass, put a join (or a node that depends on every check) between the checks and the Output, and point `config.source` at a node downstream of that join. See [[nodes/output]] and [[execution]].\n",
  "overview": "---\ntitle: 007 — TL;DR and dictionary\nsummary: Start here. What 007 is in ten lines, every node kind and MCP tool, and the index of every page.\norder: 0\n---\n\n## TL;DR\n\n- **A workflow is a graph.** One `trigger` node starts it, one `output` node names its answer, and the nodes between do the work. Edges say *when* a node may run. A node reads any upstream node's output by **ref** (`{ kind: 'ref', nodeId, path }`) — only the join-style kinds (`race`, `aggregate`, `passthrough`, …) take what their incoming edges deliver. → [[graph]]\n- **An ordinary node waits for ALL its incoming edges; `race` and `output` fire on the FIRST.** An edge from a decision arm that wasn't taken never fires, so a node joining it is skipped. `fastTrack` holds other work back until a marked path finishes. Read the rules before wiring anything with more than one incoming edge. → [[execution]]\n- **Agents are reusable and versioned.** An `agent` node points at an agent by id; the agent's config (model, prompts, tools, output contract) lives on the agent, not the node. Data reaches it only through `${variables}` the node binds, or a bound conversation. → [[agents]]\n- **References float; runs freeze.** Workflows and agents each have a draft and published versions. A node follows the latest published agent unless it pins a version; a run freezes everything it will use into its manifest at start. → [[versioning]]\n- **Two engines.** `durable` survives eviction, retries nodes and can resume; `inline` is fast, interactive and can stream. The trigger picks one for the whole run. → [[execution]]\n- **The answer can stream before anything else finishes.** On the `inline` engine, a text agent that the `output` names streams its tokens as it generates — edges into the `output` do not hold that back. To gate an answer behind checks, have the `output` name a node *after* the checks. `informUser` controls live progress per node. → [[streaming]]\n- **Evals grade agents and workflows** against samples with checks, optionally across models and prompts. → [[evals]]\n- **Through this MCP you can** read everything, and — on a write connection — create, patch and publish workflows and agents, run previews and evals. Publishing an agent is a dry run first, because it can change several workflows at once. → [[authoring]]\n- **When something went wrong**, start at `list_runs` → `get_run` → `get_run_step`. → [[debugging]]\n- **Words you don't know** are in [[concepts]]; hard numbers are in [[limits]].\n\n## How to read these docs\n\n`get_docs({ topic: \"<id>\" })` opens a page — ids are listed below, and a bare node kind (`\"race\"`) works. `get_docs({ query: \"<words>\" })` searches every page and returns the matching sections; use it for a config key (`\"continueOnError\"`) or a question (`\"wait for all\"`). Cross-references look like [[concepts]] — pass the id inside the brackets as `topic`.\n\n## Node kinds\n\nEach has a page: `get_docs({ topic: \"nodes/<kind>\" })`.\n\n{{node-kinds}}\n\n## MCP tools\n\n{{mcp-tools}}\n\n## Every page\n\n{{topics}}\n",
  "streaming": "---\ntitle: What the end user sees, and when\nsummary: Token streaming of the answer (inline engine only, answer node only), what informUser off/static/dynamic emits, the progress feed, and how to hold the answer back until checks pass.\norder: 35\n---\n\nThere are two separate channels to an end user, and a host decides how much of each to show:\n\n1. **The answer** — the value the Output node resolves to. Persisted once on the run when the Output is reached (run status `done`). On the inline engine, the answer can also stream token by token while it is being written.\n2. **The progress feed** — `progress`-level lines in the run log, persisted as they happen, which a host polls by run id.\n\n## Does the answer stream?\n\nOnly when **all** of these hold:\n\n- The workflow runs on the **inline** engine (the trigger's `engine: \"inline\"`). The durable engine never streams: a durable step boundary cannot carry a token stream.\n- The node is one that an Output node's `config.source` points at — an **answer node**. Every other node's text is never streamed, so an intermediate agent's working text cannot be mistaken for the answer.\n- The node is an agent with `output.kind: \"text\"`. Structured answers never stream.\n- The model is on a turn that **must** answer: tools are denied on that turn, or the agent has no tools. Text from a turn that might still call tools (\"Let me search…\") is not streamed, so what streams is exactly the final turn's text.\n\nWhen it streams, it starts **as soon as that agent node runs** — not when the Output resolves, and regardless of whether other branches (for example checks running in parallel) have finished. Fragments go to an in-memory buffer on the run that a host reads by cursor; they are not persisted, and a reader that attaches late sees only the final answer. The run log records `⇢ streamed N chars of the answer live` or `⇢ answer not streamed …` on the node.\n\nWhether those fragments reach a person is the **host's** choice — 007 only makes them available. A host that does not read the buffer delivers the answer whole.\n\n## `informUser`: the progress feed\n\nEvery node has `informUser` (default `{ \"mode\": \"off\" }`). It controls **only** `progress`-level log lines — never the answer, never what the agent can do:\n\n| mode | what it emits |\n| ---- | ------------- |\n| `off` | Nothing. |\n| `static` | The author's `note`, once at node start, with `${var}` filled from run variables. On an Iteration it is emitted after the list resolves, so it can use the item count. |\n| `dynamic` (agents only) | `tools: true` (default): one line per tool call, but **only for tools that declare a `statusLabel`**, interpolated from the call's arguments; other tools are silent. `reasoning: true` (default false): the model's reasoning text, one line per **finished turn** (not token by token), and only if the agent's `reasoning` is on so there is any. |\n\n- Sub-agents inherit the primary node's `informUser`.\n- Nodes **inside an Iteration** never emit progress lines (the iteration speaks for its body).\n- Every other log level — `node-start`/`node-end`, `info`, `thinking`, `tool`, `warn`, `error`, run `state` markers — is a developer feed. It is always written, regardless of `informUser`. An end-user surface should show only `progress`.\n\n## Holding everything back until checks pass\n\nPossible, with graph shape alone:\n\n1. **Do not bind the Output to the agent that writes the answer.** Add a gate node after it — for example a [[nodes/text]] node whose body is `${answer}`, with `answer` mapped to the agent's `text` — and bind the Output's `config.source` to the gate. Only the answer node streams, and a Text node does not, so nothing streams at all. The answer becomes visible only when the Output resolves.\n2. **Make the gate wait for every check.** Draw an edge into the gate from the answer agent **and from every check node**. A normal node waits for all of its live incoming edges (see [[execution]]). Do not wire the checks straight into the Output: an Output fires on the **first** live incoming edge, not all of them.\n3. **Route failures to a different ending.** Put a [[nodes/branch]] or [[nodes/switch]] on the check results before the gate, so a failed check leads to a different Output (or a different gate) and the approved Output's arm is never taken.\n4. **Keep the drafting agent quiet.** Set its `informUser` to `off` (or `dynamic` with `reasoning: false`): reasoning lines can contain the draft answer. Tool status lines are usually harmless.\n5. **Gate side effects too.** A Tool node that posts a message or sends anything does so the moment it runs; the Output does not hold it back. Give such nodes edges from the checks as well. Also, branches that do not feed the Output keep running after the answer is delivered (`done` → `completed`).\n\nIf the final delivery should still stream, make the gate itself a text agent (for example a short \"deliver this approved answer verbatim\" agent) and bind the Output to it: it runs only after its incoming edges are all satisfied, so it streams only approved text.\n\n## What the host controls vs the engine\n\n- **Engine**: which node may stream and from which turn; when the answer is final (`done`); which progress lines exist and when they are written.\n- **Host**: whether to read the token buffer at all; which log levels to show end users; how and when to post the final answer (typically when the run reaches `done`). See [[triggers]] for output contracts.\n",
  "tools": "---\ntitle: Tools\nsummary: Where tools come from, agent tools vs Tool nodes, argument validation, what a tool can read, large results, resume safety, and how tools behave in evals.\norder: 55\n---\n\nA **tool** is a registered capability with a stable id. 007 ships none of the business logic; every tool comes from one registry the host builds. Read `get_tool_catalog` for the list, and `get_tool_catalog({ toolIds })` for full input/output schemas.\n\n## Where tools come from (`origin`)\n\n- `host` — written by this deployment. The default.\n- `sdk` — shipped by 007 itself (for example text extraction, document rendering); fixed until the package is upgraded.\n- `connector` — proxied from a remote MCP server the host connected. Id shape `mcp:<connectorId>:<toolName>`, which never collides with a host id. Its read/write classification comes from the remote tool's `readOnlyHint` annotation (absent = write). Changes on the remote side need a connector refresh (`refresh_connector`, `list_connectors`).\n\nEach tool also declares (visible in the catalog): `sideEffect` (`read`/`write`/untagged — drives eval behaviour), `requiresContext` (ambient run scope the host supplies, never an argument; an empty scope usually means \"matches nothing\", not an error), and an optional `statusLabel` (the user-facing line shown when an agent calls it — see [[streaming]]).\n\n## Two ways to call a tool\n\n| | Agent tool (`toolIds` on an agent) | Tool node ([[nodes/tool]]) |\n| - | - | - |\n| who decides the arguments | the model | the graph: each arg is a literal or a `ref` |\n| which registry entries | `ai-tool` entries only | both `function` and `ai-tool` entries |\n| validation | the model's call is checked against the input schema | bound args are parsed through the input schema (defaults applied); a mismatch fails the node |\n| result goes to | the model's next turn; recorded in the step trace | the node's output, readable by downstream refs |\n\nTool-node args are written against the tool's schema field names. `validate_workflow_graph` and the editor's Issues panel flag args that no longer match the schema; ref **paths** into upstream outputs are not checked.\n\n## What a tool can read\n\nA tool sees only its arguments and the host's per-run dependencies. It cannot inspect the run. In particular:\n\n- A Tool node can read **node outputs** through `ref` bindings, nothing else.\n- An agent node's output is its answer (`{ text }` or its object). The tool calls it made, and their results, are only in the step trace — **no downstream node or tool can reference them**.\n- To let later logic see what an agent retrieved, either have the agent return it in a structured output (`output.kind: \"object\"`), or call the retrieval as Tool nodes and feed their outputs to both the agent (via `${vars}`) and the later step.\n- For checking that an agent's citations were actually seen in this run, 007 has a built-in mechanism: an agent's `referenceKinds` validates every inline reference against its prompt, messages and every tool result (including sub-agents') and strips unsourced ones. See [[agents]].\n\n## Large values\n\nA tool (or any node) may return a **blob ref** — a pointer to stored bytes — instead of a large value. Downstream nodes rehydrate it to the real value inside their own step before interpolation or argument validation, provided the host configured a blob resolver. On the durable engine, oversized node outputs are spilled automatically at the step boundary. See [[limits]].\n\n## Retries and resume\n\nA node can be re-executed: durable-engine retries replay the node body, and the inline engine resumes an interrupted run (for example after a deploy) by re-running the node that was in flight. **Tools with side effects must be idempotent** — accept a caller-supplied key or check for an existing record — or a resume can duplicate the effect. See [[execution]].\n\n## Tools in evals\n\nUnder eval simulation, per tool (keyed by tool id):\n\n- `write` tools are **always** neutralized; they return `{ simulated: true }`. The call is still recorded for grading.\n- `read` tools are `mocked` by default and return the sample's pinned output for that tool id (or `{}` if none is pinned). A sample can set a tool to `live` to hit real data.\n- **Untagged tools run for real** even under simulation — the host must tag any tool with an external effect.\n- One pinned output per tool id: every call to that tool returns it, from the primary agent and from sub-agents alike.\n- A synthesis sample stages earlier tool results as a seeded assistant turn and pins nothing; the agent keeps its tools, and calling one returns `{}`. (Older samples saved with a `frozen` mode load this same way.)\n- `run_agent_preview` is different again: all tools are simulated by the model writing plausible results.\n\n## Running one tool directly\n\n`run_tool({ toolId, args, context })` executes one catalog tool **for real**, outside any workflow — the console's tool playground. `args` are validated and defaulted by the tool's input schema; `context` fills its `requiresContext` keys. A tool tagged `write`, or untagged, is refused unless `allowWrite: true`. The call is not recorded as a run, so it does not appear in `list_tool_invocations`.\n\nSee [[evals]].\n",
  "triggers": "---\ntitle: Triggers, run input and output contracts\nsummary: How a run starts (manual, periodic, host events), what its input looks like, what its Output must satisfy, and how identity flows into child runs.\norder: 40\n---\n\n## TL;DR\n\n- Every graph has exactly one trigger node. Its `config.triggerKind` says how the workflow starts. The trigger's **output is the run's validated input**, so downstream nodes `ref` it.\n- Event triggers validate the input against the event's `inputSchema` before any node runs. They can also require the final answer to match an `outputContract`.\n- A workflow called by another workflow runs as its own **child run**, under its own trigger kind. Its input is not validated and its output contract is not enforced, because it answers to the calling node.\n\n## Trigger kinds\n\n| `triggerKind` | starts when | input |\n| ------------- | ----------- | ----- |\n| `manual` | A person starts it. | Whatever was supplied, passed through unvalidated (`{}` if none). |\n| `periodic` | On the schedule in `config.cron` (required for this kind). | Always `{}`. |\n| *an event kind* | The host fires that event. | Parsed against the event's `inputSchema`, and the run fails before any node if it does not match. |\n| `iteration_item` | Reserved for the trigger *inside* an Iteration's subgraph. | The current list item. Never startable. |\n\n`list_trigger_events` lists the host's events. For each one it gives the exact `kind` string, a description, the payload fields, the payload's JSON Schema (what you can `ref`), and the `outputContract` if there is one. `manual` and `periodic` are built in and are not in that list.\n\n007 stores and validates the cron expression, but the host is what actually fires schedules. `create_workflow` warns that a periodic workflow starts firing on its schedule once v1 is published, and v1 is published at creation.\n\nThe trigger also carries `config.engine` (`durable` / `inline`, absent means durable). It applies to the whole run. See [[execution]].\n\n## Which workflow runs for an event\n\nEach event kind is assigned to **one** workflow, a single global mapping. When the event fires, the workflow's **latest published version** runs. An **archived** workflow never runs on its event, even if it is still assigned. Assignments are managed by the host or console, not over MCP. A workflow whose trigger names an event but which is not the assigned one does not fire on it.\n\n## Starting runs from MCP\n\nThere is no \"start a run\" tool. The ways an MCP session causes executions are:\n\n- `retry_run`: re-run a finished run, either `restart` (fresh, on the latest published version) or `resume` (same version, from the failed step). **It executes real side effects.**\n- `run_eval`: runs samples against an agent or workflow as real (eval-flagged) runs. See [[evals]].\n- `run_agent_preview` / `run_decision_preview`: run one agent outside any workflow.\n\n## The run's identity\n\nA run carries opaque host references that the SDK never interprets:\n\n- `subjectId`: what the run is about.\n- `correlationId`: a grouping reference.\n- `actorId`: the person it acts for. It is empty for unattended runs.\n\nThe run can also carry run-level prompt variables, which fill `${names}` no node binds (see [[graph]]), and opaque host context. Host tools read the subject and actor from the run context, not from the graph.\n\n## Output contracts\n\nIf the trigger's event declares an `outputContract`, the value the run delivers (its first Output reached) is parsed against it. A mismatch **fails the run** with \"Workflow output does not satisfy the '<kind>' trigger contract\". Retrying will not fix it, because the binding is wrong. The editor and `validate_workflow_graph` check the Output's bound value against the contract ahead of time where they can. `manual`, `periodic` and events without a contract pass any value through.\n\nPractical rule: read `outputContract` from `list_trigger_events` *before* binding the Output's `config.source`. A chat-style event typically wants a string or `{ text }`, not a whole agent result object.\n\n## Child runs (workflow-call)\n\nA Workflow node calls another workflow. The callee:\n\n- Is its **own `wf_run`**, linked to the caller by `parentRunId` / `parentNodeId`. Its cost rolls up to the parent, and the run viewer nests it.\n- Runs on the **engine its own trigger declares**. The caller cannot override that.\n- Records **its own trigger kind**. Its subject, correlation and actor come from the callee trigger's identity resolver (if the host declared one for that kind) applied to the input the caller bound. Anything not named there is **inherited from the caller**.\n- Receives the trigger input the node built from its `inputs` bindings, or the forwarded upstream value if there are none. That input is **not validated** against the callee's `inputSchema`, and the callee's output contract is **not enforced**.\n- Is frozen into the caller's manifest at the caller's run start, at the callee's latest published version. Calls cannot pin a version.\n\nInside an *inline* iteration item, a callee cannot spawn a run of its own. It runs inline as a subgraph of that item instead. See [[nodes/workflow]].\n",
  "versioning": "---\ntitle: Drafts, versions, publishing and the run manifest\nsummary: What runs (the latest published version, frozen per run), how drafts and pins work, publishing workflows and agents over MCP, and where history lives.\norder: 80\n---\n\n## TL;DR\n\n- Workflows and agents each have **one draft** (editable, overwritten on every save) and **immutable numbered versions**. The highest published version is what runs.\n- References **float to latest**. An agent node with `config.version: null` uses the agent's newest published version, and a Workflow node always uses the callee's newest. A run **freezes** every reference into its **manifest** at run start, so a publish mid-run does not affect it.\n- **Over MCP you can publish both.** `publish_workflow` publishes a workflow draft; `publish_agent` publishes an agent draft after a mandatory dry run, because one agent publish can change several workflows.\n- A draft is not \"unsaved edits\". To see what it changes, diff it against the published version. The draft tools' replies report the delta.\n\n## Drafts\n\n- `patch_workflow_draft` (small named ops) and `update_workflow_draft` (whole graph) write the workflow draft. `update_agent_draft` writes the agent draft. All of them **replace the single draft a person may have open in the editor.**\n- Rollback is `update_workflow_draft({ fromVersion })` / `update_agent_draft({ fromVersion })`: the draft becomes an exact copy of that published version, read server-side. Nothing is published until you publish.\n- `discard_workflow_draft` / `discard_agent_draft` throw the draft away, so the editor shows the published version again.\n- What reads a draft: `run_agent_preview`, and `run_eval` with `draftAgentId` (grades an unpublished agent). Real runs never read a draft.\n\n## Publishing\n\n**Workflows.** `publish_workflow({ workflowId, baseVersionNumber, changeNote })` publishes the current draft as the next version. It is refused when:\n\n- the draft lints with any error (see `validate_workflow_graph`);\n- `baseVersionNumber` is not the version live right now (someone published since you read it; pass `0` if never published);\n- there is no draft, or the draft is identical to the live version.\n\n`changeNote` is required and becomes the version's note in history and in the change feed. An AI summary of the diff is generated as part of the publish unless you pass `summarize: false`.\n\n**Agents.** `create_agent` publishes v1 (so the agent is immediately usable). Later versions go through `publish_agent`, which takes two calls. Without `confirm` it is a dry run: it lists what changed, every published workflow that floats on the agent and will run the new version from its next run, and those pinned to an older version that won't, and returns a `confirm` token. With that token and a `changeNote` it publishes. It is refused when the token no longer matches the draft or live version, when `baseVersionNumber` is stale, when there is no draft or it equals the live version, or when the draft fails the same checks `update_agent_draft` runs. Grade the draft with `run_eval({ draftAgentId })` first.\n\n**Who published, and notes.** Each version records `publishedBy`, which is the user the session is authorized as, plus the change note and the AI summary. There is **no separate approver field**. An approver's name can only be written into the free-text `changeNote`, for workflows and agents alike.\n\n## Pinning\n\n| reference | can pin? | how |\n| --------- | -------- | --- |\n| Agent node → agent | yes | `config.version: <n>` (null floats) |\n| Sub-agent target → agent/workflow | yes | `version` on the target (see [[agents/sub-agents]]) |\n| Eval set → target | yes | the set's pinned version (null floats) |\n| Workflow node → workflow | no | always the callee's latest at run start |\n| Event → workflow | no | always the assigned workflow's latest |\n\nA pin on a version that was never published, or an `agentId` that does not exist, fails the run **before any step is recorded**. The run sits at `queued` with no logs. `validate_workflow_graph` catches both ahead of time.\n\n## The run manifest\n\nAt run start every floating reference is resolved, transitively through called workflows and sub-agent targets, to `{ id, versionId, versionNumber, config|graph }`, and stored on the run. Consequences:\n\n- A run is reproducible from its own row, even after its agents change or are archived.\n- Republishing an agent does not affect runs already in flight, but changes every run started after.\n- `retry_run` `restart` resolves a **new** manifest (latest versions), while `resume` keeps the original version.\n- Eval drift has two axes. A sample's hash can't see that its floating target was republished, so `get_eval_run` reports that separately (`agentRepublishedSinceLastRun`). See [[evals]].\n\n## History and audit\n\n- `list_workflow_versions` / `list_agent_versions` list each version's number, note and AI summary, newest first. `get_workflow_version` reads one graph. `list_agent_versions({ versionNumber })` reads one config.\n- `list_changes` reads the **change log**, the only who-touched-what record. It covers workflows, agents, eval sets and samples, models, trigger assignments, connectors and connector tools, with the actions create / update / publish / archive / restore / assign / enable / disable. Each entry carries an actor and a source (`ui`, `mcp`, `spec-import`, `system`).\n- Archiving (`update_workflow` / the console) retires an entity without deleting it. An archived workflow never runs on its event. An agent cannot be archived while a live workflow still references it.\n- The editor's undo is per browser tab and does not cover MCP edits. From MCP, \"undo\" means `fromVersion` or a discard.\n\n## Moving definitions between environments\n\nThe SDK's `wf-spec` CLI exports and imports agents, workflows and eval sets as version-controlled JSON files keyed by **slug**, a stable identity that survives renames, unlike ids, which differ per database. Drafts and run history are not part of the spec. Import publishes changed entities as new versions.\n",
}
